INSERT INTO documents VALUES('9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','Cinderella.pdf','Cinderella','sources/','sources/Cinderella.pdf','source','pdf',32094,1,'ready',5,unistr('# Cinderella, or the Little Glass Slipper\u000a\u000aOnce there was a gentleman who married, for his second wife, the proudest and most haughty woman that was ever seen. She had, by a former husband, two daughters of her own humor, who were, indeed, exactly like her in all things. He had likewise, by another wife, a young daughter, but of unparalleled goodness and sweetness of temper, which she took from her mother, who was the best creature in the world.\u000a\u000aNo sooner were the ceremonies of the wedding over but the mother-in-law began to show herself in her true colors. She could not bear the good qualities of this pretty girl, and the less because they made her own daughters appear the more odious. She employed her in the meanest work of the house: she scoured the dishes, tables, etc., and scrubbed madam’s chamber, and those of misses, her daughters; she lay up in a sorry garret, upon a wretched straw bed, while her sisters lay in fine rooms, with floors all inlaid, upon beds of the very newest fashion, and where they had looking-glasses so large that they might see themselves at their full length from head to foot.\u000a\u000aThe poor girl bore all patiently, and dared not tell her father, who would have rattled her off; for his wife governed him entirely. When she had done her work, she used to go into the chimney-corner, and sit down among cinders and ashes, which made her commonly be called Cinderwench; but the youngest, who was not so rude and uncivil as the eldest, called her Cinderella. However, Cinderella, notwithstanding her mean apparel, was a hundred times handsomer than her sisters, though they were always dressed very richly.\u000a\u000aIt happened that the King’s son gave a ball, and invited all persons of fashion to it. Our young misses were also invited, for they cut a very grand figure among the quality. They were mightily delighted at this invitation, and wonderfully busy in choosing out such gowns, petticoats, and head-clothes as might become them. This was a new trouble to Cinderella; for it was she who ironed her sisters’ linen, and plaited their ruffles; they talked all day long of nothing but how they should be dressed.\u000a\u000a“For my part,” said the eldest, “I will wear my red velvet suit with French trimming.”\u000a\u000a“And I,” said the youngest, “shall have my usual petticoat; but then, to make amends for that, I will put on my gold-flowered manteau, and my diamond stomacher, which is far from being the most ordinary one in the world.”\u000a\u000aThey sent for the best tire-woman they could get to make up their head-dresses and adjust their double pinners, and they had their red brushes and patches from Mademoiselle de la Poche.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000aCinderella was likewise called up to them to be consulted in all these matters, for she had excellent notions, and advised them always for the best, nay, and offered her services to dress their heads, which they were very willing she should do. As she was doing this, they said to her:\u000a\u000a“Cinderella, would you not be glad to go to the ball?” “Alas!” said she, “you only jeer me; it is not for such as I am to go thither.” “Thou art in the right of it,” replied they; “it would make the people laugh to see a Cinderwench at a ball.”\u000a\u000aAnyone but Cinderella would have dressed their heads awry, but she was very good, and dressed them perfectly well They were almost two days without eating, so much were they transported with joy. They broke above a dozen laces in trying to be laced up close, that they might have a fine slender shape, and they were continually at their looking-glass. At last the happy day came; they went to Court, and Cinderella followed them with her eyes as long as she could, and when she had lost sight of them, she fell a-crying.\u000a\u000aHer godmother, who saw her all in tears, asked her what was the matter. “I wish I could–I wish I could–“; she was not able to speak the rest, being interrupted by her tears and sobbing. This godmother of hers, who was a fairy, said to her, “Thou wishest thou couldst go to the ball; is it not so?” “Y–es,” cried Cinderella, with a great sigh.\u000a\u000a“Well,” said her godmother, “be but a good girl, and I will contrive that thou shalt go.” Then she took her into her chamber, and said to her, “Run into the garden, and bring me a pumpkin.”\u000a\u000aCinderella went immediately to gather the finest she could get, and brought it to her godmother, not being able to imagine how this pumpkin could make her go to the ball. Her godmother scooped out all the inside of it, having left nothing but the rind; which done, she struck it with her wand, and the pumpkin was instantly turned into a fine coach, gilded all over with gold.\u000a\u000aShe then went to look into her mouse-trap, where she found six mice, all alive, and ordered Cinderella to lift up a little the trapdoor, when, giving each mouse, as it went out, a little tap with her wand, the mouse was that moment turned into a fine horse, which altogether made a very fine set of six horses of a beautiful mouse-colored dapple-gray. Being at a loss for a coachman,\u000a\u000a“I will go and see,” says Cinderella, “if there is never a rat in the rat-trap–we may make a coachman of him.”\u000a\u000a“Thou art in the right,” replied her godmother; “go and look.”\u000a\u000aCinderella brought the trap to her, and in it there were three huge rats. The fairy made choice of one of the three which had the largest beard, and, having touched him with her\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000awand, he was turned into a fat, jolly coachman, who had the smartest whiskers eyes ever beheld. After that, she said to her:\u000a\u000a“Go again into the garden, and you will find six lizards behind the watering-pot, bring them to me.”\u000a\u000aShe had no sooner done so but her godmother turned them into six footmen, who skipped up immediately behind the coach, with their liveries all bedaubed with gold and silver, and clung as close behind each other as if they had done nothing else their whole lives. The Fairy then said to Cinderella:\u000a\u000a“Well, you see here an equipage fit to go to the ball with; are you not pleased with it?” “Oh! yes,” cried she; “but must I go thither as I am, in these nasty rags?”\u000a\u000aHer godmother only just touched her with her wand, and, at the same instant, her clothes were turned into cloth of gold and silver, all beset with jewels. This done, she gave her a pair of glass slippers, the prettiest in the whole world. Being thus decked out, she got up into her coach; but her godmother, above all things, commanded her not to stay till after midnight, telling her, at the same time, that if she stayed one moment longer, the coach would be a pumpkin again, her horses mice, her coachman a rat, her footmen lizards, and her clothes become just as they were before.\u000a\u000aShe promised her godmother she would not fail of leaving the ball before midnight; and then away she drives, scarce able to contain herself for joy. The King’s son who was told that a great princess, whom nobody knew, was come, ran out to receive her; he gave her his hand\u000a\u000a- as she alighted out of the coach, and led her into the ball, among all the company. There was immediately a profound silence, they left off dancing, and the violins ceased to play, so attentive was everyone to contemplate the singular beauties of the unknown new-comer. Nothing was then heard but a confused noise of: “Ha! how handsome she is! Ha! how handsome she is!”\u000a\u000aThe King himself, old as he was, could not help watching her, and telling the Queen softly that it was a long time since he had seen so beautiful and lovely a creature.\u000a\u000aAll the ladies were busied in considering her clothes and headdress, that they might have some made next day after the same pattern, provided they could meet with such fine material and as able hands to make them.\u000a\u000aThe King’s son conducted her to the most honorable seat, and afterward took her out to dance with him; she danced so very gracefully that they all more and more admired her. A fine collation was served up, whereof the young prince ate not a morsel, so intently was he busied in gazing on her.\u000a\u000aShe went and sat down by her sisters, showing them a thousand civilities, giving them part of the oranges and citrons which the Prince had presented her with, which very much surprised them, for they did not know her. While Cinderella was thus amusing her sisters, she heard the clock strike eleven and three-quarters, whereupon she immediately made a courtesy to the company and hasted away as fast as she could.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000aWhen she got home she ran to seek out her godmother, and, after having thanked her, she said she could not but heartily wish she might go next day to the ball, because the King’s son had desired her.\u000a\u000aAs she was eagerly telling her godmother whatever had passed at the ball, her two sisters knocked at the door, which Cinderella ran and opened.\u000a\u000a“How long you have stayed!” cried she, gaping, rubbing her eyes and stretching herself as if she had been just waked out of her sleep; she had not, however, any manner of inclination to sleep since they went from home.\u000a\u000a“If thou hadst been at the ball,” said one of her sisters, “thou wouldst not have been tired with it. There came thither the finest princess, the most beautiful ever was seen with mortal eyes; she showed us a thousand civilities, and gave us oranges and citrons.”\u000a\u000aCinderella seemed very indifferent in the matter; indeed, she asked them the name of that princess; but they told her they did not know it, and that the King’s son was very uneasy on her account and would give all the world to know who she was. At this Cinderella, smiling, replied:\u000a\u000a“She must, then, be very beautiful indeed; how happy you have been! Could not I see her? Ah! dear Miss Charlotte, do lend me your yellow suit of clothes which you wear every day.” “Ay, to be sure!” cried Miss Charlotte; “lend my clothes to such a dirty Cinderwench as thou art! I should be a fool.” Cinderella, indeed, expected well such answer, and was very glad of the refusal; for she would have been sadly put to it if her sister had lent her what she asked for jestingly.\u000a\u000aThe next day the two sisters were at the ball, and so was Cinderella, but dressed more magnificently than before. The King’s son was always by her, and never ceased his compliments and kind speeches to her; to whom all this was so far from being tiresome that she quite forgot what her godmother had recommended to her; so that she, at last, counted the clock striking twelve when she took it to be no more than eleven; she then rose up and fled, as nimble as a deer. The Prince followed, but could not overtake her. She left behind one of her glass slippers, which the Prince took up most carefully. She got home but quite out of breath, and in her nasty old clothes, having nothing left her of all her finery but one of the little slippers, fellow to that she dropped. The guards at the palace gate were asked:\u000a\u000aIf they had not seen a princess go out. Who said: They had seen nobody go out but a young girl, very meanly dressed, and who had more the air of a poor country wench than a gentlewoman. When the two sisters returned from the ball Cinderella asked them: If they had been well diverted, and if the fine lady had been there.\u000a\u000aThey told her: Yes, but that she hurried away immediately when it struck twelve, and with so much haste that she dropped one of her little glass slippers, the prettiest in the world, which the King’s son had taken up; that he had done nothing but look at her all the time\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a- at the ball, and that most certainly he was very much in love with the beautiful person who owned the glass slipper.\u000a\u000aWhat they said was very true; for a few days after the King’s son caused it to be proclaimed, by sound of trumpet, that he would marry her whose foot the slipper would just fit. They whom he employed began to try it upon the princesses, then the duchesses and all the Court, but in vain; it was brought to the two sisters, who did all they possibly could to thrust their foot into the slipper, but they could not effect it. Cinderella, who saw all this, and knew her slipper, said to them, laughing:\u000a\u000a“Let me see if it will not fit me.” Her sisters burst out a-laughing, and began to banter her. The gentleman who was sent to try the slipper looked earnestly at Cinderella, and, finding her very handsome, said: It was but just that she should try, and that he had orders to let everyone make trial.\u000a\u000aHe obliged Cinderella to sit down, and, putting the slipper to her foot, he found it went on very easily, and fitted her as if it had been made of wax. The astonishment her two sisters were in was excessively great, but still abundantly greater when Cinderella pulled out of her pocket the other slipper, and put it on her foot. Thereupon, in came her godmother, who, having touched with her wand Cinderella’s clothes, made them richer and more magnificent than any of those she had before.\u000a\u000aAnd now her two sisters found her to be that fine, beautiful lady whom they had seen at the ball. They threw themselves at her feet to beg pardon for all the ill-treatment they had made her undergo. Cinderella took them up, and, as she embraced them, cried:\u000a\u000aThat she forgave them with all her heart, and desired them always to love her.\u000a\u000aShe was conducted to the young prince, dressed as she was; he thought her more charming than ever, and, a few days after, married her. Cinderella, who was no less good than beautiful, gave her two sisters lodgings in the palace, and that very same day matched them with two great lords of the Court.(1)\u000a\u000a(1) Charles Perrault.\u000a\u000aPublic domain. From The Blue Fairy Book (ed. Andrew Lang, 1889); Project Gutenberg ebook #503.'),'[]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'opendataloader','136be97b2925ec525918c39a3bbe2a913c5da647037bb65241540ea5b0729911',1780002789047311744,'2026-05-28 21:13:09',NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:09','2026-05-28 21:13:10',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('ffdca276-4f06-4195-a43e-5bb1fd3cb3c7','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','cinderella.md','Cinderella','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/cinderella.md','wiki','md',0,2,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Cinderella.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Cinderella\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aCinderella is a central character in a well-known fairy tale, symbolizing resilience and virtue amidst adversity. Her story highlights themes of kindness, hope, and transformation.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Represents resilience in overcoming hardship.\u000a- Embodies virtues such as kindness and humility.\u000a- Often depicted as mistreated by her stepfamily.\u000a- Experiences a significant transformation through magical intervention.\u000a- Ultimately rewarded for her goodness and perseverance.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aCinderella is a prominent figure in folklore and fairy tales, often serving as an archetype for the "rags to riches" narrative. Her story has been adapted into various cultural forms, including literature, theater, and film, influencing the portrayal of female protagonists in literature and popular media.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Cinderella.pdf'),'["entity"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:18','2026-05-28 21:13:18',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('e834a6f0-141e-459f-9b25-25a4b99b1b59','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','fairy-godmother.md','Fairy Godmother','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/fairy-godmother.md','wiki','md',0,3,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Cinderella.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Fairy Godmother\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Fairy Godmother is a magical figure in folklore who provides assistance to the protagonist, often in times of need. In the context of the story of Cinderella, she symbolizes hope and the transformative power of kindness.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Magical abilities to grant wishes or provide assistance.\u000a- Acts as a mentor or guide to the protagonist.\u000a- Represents themes of hope, kindness, and transformation.\u000a- Often depicted as a benevolent and nurturing figure.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe concept of the Fairy Godmother is prevalent in various fairy tales and folklore, serving as a narrative device to highlight the importance of kindness and the potential for change in one''s circumstances. In the broader domain of fairy tales, she embodies the archetype of the helper, reinforcing the idea that good deeds and compassion can lead to positive outcomes.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Cinderella.pdf'),'["entity"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:21','2026-05-28 21:13:21',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('c8f483c8-6188-4716-bfe1-f553b98c7946','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','glass-slipper.md','Glass Slipper','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/glass-slipper.md','wiki','md',0,4,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [instrument]\u000asources: [Cinderella.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Glass Slipper\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe glass slipper is a magical footwear item featured in the fairy tale "Cinderella," serving as a crucial plot device that enables Cinderella''s identification and subsequent marriage to the prince.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Made of glass, symbolizing purity and beauty.\u000a- Fits only Cinderella, highlighting her uniqueness.\u000a- Represents transformation and the fulfillment of dreams.\u000a- Acts as a catalyst for the resolution of the story.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe glass slipper is emblematic of the broader themes of fairy tales, including the triumph of good over evil, the importance of identity, and the transformative power of love. It underscores the narrative''s focus on self-discovery and the idea that true worth is recognized through genuine connection.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Cinderella.pdf'),'["instrument"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:23','2026-05-28 21:13:23',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('88ff625f-5833-4549-9800-11493b389186','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','transformation.md','Transformation','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/transformation.md','wiki','md',0,5,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [Cinderella.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Transformation\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aTransformation refers to a significant change in form, appearance, or character. In the context of storytelling, it often symbolizes personal growth or a shift in circumstances.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Represents a shift from a lower to a higher status, as seen in Cinderella''s journey from oppression to royalty.\u000a- Often involves external changes (e.g., physical appearance, social status) that reflect internal growth or change.\u000a- Can be triggered by specific events or interventions, such as magical assistance or personal resilience.\u000a- Highlights themes of hope, redemption, and the possibility of change.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe theme of transformation is a common motif in literature, illustrating the potential for change in characters'' lives. In "Cinderella," it serves to emphasize the contrast between despair and hope, showcasing the journey from hardship to fulfillment. This theme resonates with audiences by reflecting universal desires for improvement and the belief in the possibility of a better future.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Cinderella.pdf'),'["theme"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:31','2026-05-28 21:13:31',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('a4bab150-df3c-4f26-926d-a7ba78360039','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','forgiveness.md','Forgiveness','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/forgiveness.md','wiki','md',0,6,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [Cinderella.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Forgiveness\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aForgiveness is the process of letting go of resentment or anger towards someone who has caused harm or distress. In the context of storytelling, it often reflects a character''s moral strength and capacity for compassion.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Involves the release of negative emotions such as anger and resentment.\u000a- Often requires a sense of empathy or understanding towards the offender.\u000a- Can lead to personal growth and healing for the forgiver.\u000a- May be depicted as a noble or virtuous act in literature and narratives.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aForgiveness is a prevalent theme in literature and moral teachings, often serving as a catalyst for character development and resolution of conflict. In "Cinderella," the protagonist''s ability to forgive her stepsisters for their mistreatment highlights her noble character and reinforces the idea that forgiveness can lead to reconciliation and personal dignity.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Cinderella.pdf'),'["theme"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:35','2026-05-28 21:13:35',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('db7750bd-01f2-44fe-921e-407c2296d6f1','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','cinderella.md','Cinderella','/wiki/summaries/','wiki/summaries/cinderella.md','wiki','md',0,7,'ready',NULL,unistr('# Cinderella\u000a\u000a**Source:** Cinderella.pdf | **Type:** pdf | **Pages:** 5 | **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a\u000a## Summary\u000a\u000aThe story of Cinderella follows a young girl mistreated by her stepmother and stepsisters who, with the help of her fairy godmother, attends a royal ball and captures the heart of the prince. After losing a glass slipper during her hurried departure, the prince searches for her, ultimately leading to her recognition and a happy ending. The tale emphasizes themes of kindness, transformation, and forgiveness.\u000a\u000a## Related Concepts\u000a\u000a- [Cinderella](../concepts/cinderella.md)\u000a- [Fairy Godmother](../concepts/fairy-godmother.md)\u000a- [Glass Slipper](../concepts/glass-slipper.md)\u000a- [Transformation](../concepts/transformation.md)\u000a- [Forgiveness](../concepts/forgiveness.md)\u000a\u000a## Source Information\u000a\u000a- **File:** Cinderella.pdf\u000a- **Type:** pdf\u000a- **Pages:** 5\u000a- **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a- **Parser:** opendataloader\u000a\u000a[^1]: Cinderella.pdf\u000a'),'[]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:35','2026-05-28 21:13:35','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3');
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('fd3ae084-fbd2-4de8-a63a-4c671574d943','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','Little Red Riding Hood.pdf','Little Red Riding Hood','sources/','sources/Little Red Riding Hood.pdf','source','pdf',18970,8,'ready',2,unistr('# Little Red Riding Hood\u000a\u000aOnce upon a time there lived in a certain village a little country girl, the prettiest creature was ever seen. Her mother was excessively fond of her; and her grandmother doted on her still more. This good woman had made for her a little red riding-hood; which became the girl so extremely well that everybody called her Little Red Riding-Hood.\u000a\u000aOne day her mother, having made some custards, said to her: “Go, my dear, and see how thy grandmamma does, for I hear she has been very ill; carry her a custard, and this little pot of butter.” Little Red Riding-Hood set out immediately to go to her grandmother, who lived in another village.\u000a\u000aAs she was going through the wood, she met with Gaffer Wolf, who had a very great mind to eat her up, but he dared not, because of some faggot-makers hard by in the forest. He asked her whither she was going. The poor child, who did not know that it was dangerous to stay and hear a wolf talk, said to him:\u000a\u000a“I am going to see my grandmamma and carry her a custard and a little pot of butter from my mamma.”\u000a\u000a“Does she live far off?” said the Wolf. “Oh! ay,” answered Little Red Riding-Hood; “it is beyond that mill you see there, at the first house in the village.” “Well,” said the Wolf, “and I’ll go and see her too. I’ll go this way and you go that, and we shall see who will be there soonest.”\u000a\u000aThe Wolf began to run as fast as he could, taking the nearest way, and the little girl went by that farthest about, diverting herself in gathering nuts, running after butterflies, and making nosegays of such little flowers as she met with. The Wolf was not long before he got to the old woman’s house. He knocked at the door–tap, tap.\u000a\u000a“Who’s there?” “Your grandchild, Little Red Riding-Hood,” replied the Wolf, counterfeiting her voice; “who has brought you a custard and a little pot of butter sent you by mamma.” The good grandmother, who was in bed, because she was somewhat ill, cried out:\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a“Pull the bobbin, and the latch will go up.”\u000a\u000aThe Wolf pulled the bobbin, and the door opened, and then presently he fell upon the good woman and ate her up in a moment, for it was above three days that he had not touched a bit. He then shut the door and went into the grandmother’s bed, expecting Little Red Riding-Hood, who came some time afterward and knocked at the door–tap, tap.\u000a\u000a“Who’s there?” Little Red Riding-Hood, hearing the big voice of the Wolf, was at first afraid; but believing her grandmother had got a cold and was hoarse, answered: “‘Tis your grandchild, Little Red Riding-Hood, who has brought you a custard and a little pot of butter mamma sends you.” The Wolf cried out to her, softening his voice as much as he could: “Pull the bobbin, and the latch will go up.” Little Red Riding-Hood pulled the bobbin, and the door opened. The Wolf, seeing her come in, said to her, hiding himself under the bed-clothes: “Put the custard and the little pot of butter upon the stool, and come and lie down with me.” Little Red Riding-Hood undressed herself and went into bed, where, being greatly amazed to see how her grandmother looked in her night-clothes, she said to her: “Grandmamma, what great arms you have got!” “That is the better to hug thee, my dear.” “Grandmamma, what great legs you have got!” “That is to run the better, my child.” “Grandmamma, what great ears you have got!” “That is to hear the better, my child.” “Grandmamma, what great eyes you have got!” “It is to see the better, my child.” “Grandmamma, what great teeth you have got!” “That is to eat thee up.” And, saying these words, this wicked wolf fell upon Little Red Riding-Hood, and ate her all up.\u000a\u000aPublic domain. From The Blue Fairy Book (ed. Andrew Lang, 1889); Project Gutenberg ebook #503.'),'[]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'opendataloader','9bb2e6242ac6b6541a1022c7fad59355d67f8253bb953a1f1d48eb13d53eede2',1780002789047835741,'2026-05-28 21:13:40',NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:40','2026-05-28 21:13:40',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('bb1e9772-ad9b-428c-9ed7-793b234c7ca7','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','little-red-riding-hood.md','Little Red Riding Hood','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/little-red-riding-hood.md','wiki','md',0,9,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Little Red Riding Hood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Little Red Riding Hood\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aLittle Red Riding Hood is a classic fairy tale character recognized for her iconic red cloak and the moral lessons that arise from her interactions with a wolf.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Wears a distinctive red cloak.\u000a- Represents innocence and naivety.\u000a- Encounters a wolf that symbolizes danger and deception.\u000a- The story often conveys moral lessons about caution and the consequences of disobedience.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aLittle Red Riding Hood is part of the broader tradition of fairy tales that often feature anthropomorphic characters and moral lessons. The tale has been adapted and retold in various cultures, highlighting themes of vulnerability and the importance of heeding warnings.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Little Red Riding Hood.pdf'),'["entity"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:47','2026-05-28 21:13:47',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('1a9107f8-3cfb-418d-9c56-0cb73137ea05','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','the-wolf.md','The Wolf','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/the-wolf.md','wiki','md',0,10,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Little Red Riding Hood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# The Wolf\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Wolf is a character in the fairy tale "Little Red Riding Hood," symbolizing cunning and predatory behavior. As the antagonist, the Wolf deceives and ultimately consumes both Little Red Riding Hood and her grandmother.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Represents cunning and deceitfulness.\u000a- Acts as the primary antagonist in the story.\u000a- Engages in manipulation to achieve its goals.\u000a- Embodies predatory instincts, preying on the innocent.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe Wolf serves as a critical figure in the narrative of "Little Red Riding Hood," illustrating themes of danger and the consequences of naivety. This character reflects broader archetypes of villainy in folklore, where predators often symbolize the threats that exist in the world, particularly those that target the vulnerable.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Little Red Riding Hood.pdf'),'["entity"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:50','2026-05-28 21:13:50',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('acfc1927-f028-476f-ac4a-89bf82df1eb3','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','cautionary-tales.md','Cautionary Tales','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/cautionary-tales.md','wiki','md',0,11,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [Little Red Riding Hood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Cautionary Tales\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aCautionary tales are narratives that serve to warn readers about specific dangers or moral lessons, often highlighting the consequences of certain behaviors or decisions. They typically aim to instill a sense of caution and awareness in the audience.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- **Moral Lesson**: Each tale conveys a clear moral or lesson, often related to safety or ethical behavior.\u000a- **Warning Against Naivety**: Many cautionary tales emphasize the dangers of being naive or trusting, particularly in unfamiliar situations.\u000a- **Consequences**: The stories often depict negative outcomes that result from ignoring the warnings, reinforcing the importance of caution.\u000a- **Engaging Narrative**: They are typically engaging and memorable, using vivid characters and scenarios to illustrate their points.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aCautionary tales are a significant aspect of folklore and literature, often used in educational settings to teach children about safety and morality. They relate to broader themes of social norms and values, serving as tools for cultural transmission and behavioral guidance.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Little Red Riding Hood.pdf'),'["theme"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:53','2026-05-28 21:13:53',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('f1f3bead-456d-47a6-a554-c0ef06790fc3','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','fairy-tales.md','Fairy Tales','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/fairy-tales.md','wiki','md',0,12,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [Little Red Riding Hood.pdf, The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Fairy Tales\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aFairy tales are a genre of folklore that typically feature magical elements, fantastical creatures, and moral lessons. They often reflect cultural values and societal norms through their narratives.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Inclusion of magical or supernatural elements\u000a- Moral lessons or ethical dilemmas\u000a- Simplistic characters, often archetypal (e.g., heroes, villains)\u000a- A clear distinction between good and evil\u000a- Settings that are often in a mythical or fantastical realm\u000a- Use of repetition and rhyme in storytelling\u000a- Frequently feature fantastical creatures such as fairies, ogres, and enchanted beings\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aFairy tales are part of a broader tradition of storytelling that spans cultures and generations. They serve not only as entertainment but also as a means to convey important life lessons and cultural values. The story of "Little Red Riding Hood" exemplifies these characteristics, illustrating the dangers of naivety and the importance of caution, while also reflecting societal norms regarding trust and the nature of strangers.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Little Red Riding Hood.pdf\u000a- The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf\u000a---'),'["theme"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:56','2026-05-28 21:14:09',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('eb4e247c-475a-4530-813b-85fc70cb4a11','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','little-red-riding-hood.md','Little Red Riding Hood','/wiki/summaries/','wiki/summaries/little-red-riding-hood.md','wiki','md',0,13,'ready',NULL,unistr('# Little Red Riding Hood\u000a\u000a**Source:** Little Red Riding Hood.pdf | **Type:** pdf | **Pages:** 2 | **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a\u000a## Summary\u000a\u000aThe story of Little Red Riding Hood follows a young girl who is sent by her mother to visit her sick grandmother, carrying custards and butter. On her way, she encounters a wolf who tricks her and ultimately devours both her and her grandmother. The tale serves as a cautionary narrative about the dangers of naivety and the importance of being wary of strangers.\u000a\u000a## Related Concepts\u000a\u000a- [Little Red Riding Hood](../concepts/little-red-riding-hood.md)\u000a- [The Wolf](../concepts/the-wolf.md)\u000a- [Cautionary Tales](../concepts/cautionary-tales.md)\u000a- [Fairy Tales](../concepts/fairy-tales.md)\u000a\u000a## Source Information\u000a\u000a- **File:** Little Red Riding Hood.pdf\u000a- **Type:** pdf\u000a- **Pages:** 2\u000a- **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a- **Parser:** opendataloader\u000a\u000a[^1]: Little Red Riding Hood.pdf\u000a'),'[]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:56','2026-05-28 21:13:56','fd3ae084-fbd2-4de8-a63a-4c671574d943');
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf','The Sleeping Beauty In The Wood','sources/','sources/The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf','source','pdf',39559,14,'ready',7,unistr('# The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood\u000a\u000aThere were formerly a king and a queen, who were so sorry that they had no children; so sorry that it cannot be expressed. They went to all the waters in the world; vows, pilgrimages, all ways were tried, and all to no purpose.\u000a\u000aAt last, however, the Queen had a daughter. There was a very fine christening; and the Princess had for her god-mothers all the fairies they could find in the whole kingdom (they found seven), that every one of them might give her a gift, as was the custom of fairies in those days. By this means the Princess had all the perfections imaginable.\u000a\u000aAfter the ceremonies of the christening were over, all the company returned to the King’s palace, where was prepared a great feast for the fairies. There was placed before every one of them a magnificent cover with a case of massive gold, wherein were a spoon, knife, and fork, all of pure gold set with diamonds and rubies. But as they were all sitting down at table they saw come into the hall a very old fairy, whom they had not invited, because it was above fifty years since she had been out of a certain tower, and she was believed to be either dead or enchanted.\u000a\u000aThe King ordered her a cover, but could not furnish her with a case of gold as the others, because they had only seven made for the seven fairies. The old Fairy fancied she was slighted, and muttered some threats between her teeth. One of the young fairies who sat by her overheard how she grumbled; and, judging that she might give the little Princess some unlucky gift, went, as soon as they rose from table, and hid herself behind the hangings, that she might speak last, and repair, as much as she could, the evil which the old Fairy might intend.\u000a\u000aIn the meanwhile all the fairies began to give their gifts to the Princess. The youngest gave her for gift that she should be the most beautiful person in the world; the next, that she should have the wit of an angel; the third, that she should have a wonderful grace in everything she did; the fourth, that she should dance perfectly well; the fifth, that she should sing like a nightingale; and the sixth, that she should play all kinds of music to the utmost perfection.\u000a\u000aThe old Fairy’s turn coming next, with a head shaking more with spite than age, she said that the Princess should have her hand pierced with a spindle and die of the wound. This terrible gift made the whole company tremble, and everybody fell a-crying.\u000a\u000aAt this very instant the young Fairy came out from behind the hangings, and spake these words aloud:\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a“Assure yourselves, O King and Queen, that your daughter shall not die of this disaster. It is true, I have no power to undo entirely what my elder has done. The Princess shall indeed pierce her hand with a spindle; but, instead of dying, she shall only fall into a profound sleep, which shall last a hundred years, at the expiration of which a king’s son shall come and awake her.”\u000a\u000aThe King, to avoid the misfortune foretold by the old Fairy, caused immediately proclamation to be made, whereby everybody was forbidden, on pain of death, to spin with a distaff and spindle, or to have so much as any spindle in their houses. About fifteen or sixteen years after, the King and Queen being gone to one of their houses of pleasure, the young Princess happened one day to divert herself in running up and down the palace; when going up from one apartment to another, she came into a little room on the top of the tower, where a good old woman, alone, was spinning with her spindle. This good woman had never heard of the King’s proclamation against spindles.\u000a\u000a“What are you doing there, goody?” said the Princess. “I am spinning, my pretty child,” said the old woman, who did not know who she was. “Ha!” said the Princess, “this is very pretty; how do you do it? Give it to me, that I may see if I can do so.”\u000a\u000aShe had no sooner taken it into her hand than, whether being very hasty at it, somewhat unhandy, or that the decree of the Fairy had so ordained it, it ran into her hand, and she fell down in a swoon.\u000a\u000aThe good old woman, not knowing very well what to do in this affair, cried out for help. People came in from every quarter in great numbers; they threw water upon the Princess’s face, unlaced her, struck her on the palms of her hands, and rubbed her temples with Hungarywater; but nothing would bring her to herself.\u000a\u000aAnd now the King, who came up at the noise, bethought himself of the prediction of the fairies, and, judging very well that this must necessarily come to pass, since the fairies had said it, caused the Princess to be carried into the finest apartment in his palace, and to be laid upon a bed all embroidered with gold and silver.\u000a\u000aOne would have taken her for a little angel, she was so very beautiful; for her swooning away had not diminished one bit of her complexion; her cheeks were carnation, and her lips were coral; indeed, her eyes were shut, but she was heard to breathe softly, which satisfied those about her that she was not dead. The King commanded that they should not disturb her, but let her sleep quietly till her hour of awaking was come.\u000a\u000aThe good Fairy who had saved her life by condemning her to sleep a hundred years was in the kingdom of Matakin, twelve thousand leagues off, when this accident befell the Princess; but she was instantly informed of it by a little dwarf, who had boots of seven leagues, that is, boots with which he could tread over seven leagues of ground in one stride. The Fairy came away immediately, and she arrived, about an hour after, in a fiery chariot drawn by dragons.\u000a\u000aThe King handed her out of the chariot, and she approved everything he had done, but as she had very great foresight, she thought when the Princess should awake she might not know\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000awhat to do with herself, being all alone in this old palace; and this was what she did: she touched with her wand everything in the palace (except the King and Queen)–governesses, maids of honor, ladies of the bedchamber, gentlemen, oﬀicers, stewards, cooks, undercooks, scullions, guards, with their beefeaters, pages, footmen; she likewise touched all the horses which were in the stables, pads as well as others, the great dogs in the outward court and pretty little Mopsey too, the Princess’s little spaniel, which lay by her on the bed.\u000a\u000aImmediately upon her touching them they all fell asleep, that they might not awake before their mistress and that they might be ready to wait upon her when she wanted them. The very spits at the fire, as full as they could hold of partridges and pheasants, did fall asleep also. All this was done in a moment. Fairies are not long in doing their business.\u000a\u000aAnd now the King and the Queen, having kissed their dear child without waking her, went out of the palace and put forth a proclamation that nobody should dare to come near it.\u000a\u000aThis, however, was not necessary, for in a quarter of an hour’s time there grew up all round about the park such a vast number of trees, great and small, bushes and brambles, twining one within another, that neither man nor beast could pass through; so that nothing could be seen but the very top of the towers of the palace; and that, too, not unless it was a good way off. Nobody; doubted but the Fairy gave herein a very extraordinary sample of her art, that the Princess, while she continued sleeping, might have nothing to fear from any curious people.\u000a\u000aWhen a hundred years were gone and passed the son of the King then reigning, and who was of another family from that of the sleeping Princess, being gone a-hunting on that side of the country, asked:\u000a\u000aWhat those towers were which he saw in the middle of a great thick wood? Everyone answered according as they had heard. Some said: That it was a ruinous old castle, haunted by spirits. Others, That all the sorcerers and witches of the country kept there their sabbath or night’s meeting.\u000a\u000aThe common opinion was: That an ogre lived there, and that he carried thither all the little children he could catch, that he might eat them up at his leisure, without anybody being able to follow him, as having himself only the power to pass through the wood.\u000a\u000aThe Prince was at a stand, not knowing what to believe, when a very good countryman spake to him thus:\u000a\u000a“May it please your royal highness, it is now about fifty years since I heard from my father, who heard my grandfather say, that there was then in this castle a princess, the most beautiful was ever seen; that she must sleep there a hundred years, and should be waked by a king’s son, for whom she was reserved.”\u000a\u000aThe young Prince was all on fire at these words, believing, without weighing the matter, that he could put an end to this rare adventure; and, pushed on by love and honor, resolved that moment to look into it.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000aScarce had he advanced toward the wood when all the great trees, the bushes, and brambles gave way of themselves to let him pass through; he walked up to the castle which he saw at the end of a large avenue which he went into; and what a little surprised him was that he saw none of his people could follow him, because the trees closed again as soon as he had passed through them. However, he did not cease from continuing his way; a young and amorous prince is always valiant.\u000a\u000aHe came into a spacious outward court, where everything he saw might have frozen the most fearless person with horror. There reigned all over a most frightful silence; the image of death everywhere showed itself, and there was nothing to be seen but stretched-out bodies of men and animals, all seeming to be dead. He, however, very well knew, by the ruby faces and pimpled noses of the beefeaters, that they were only asleep; and their goblets, wherein still remained some drops of wine, showed plainly that they fell asleep in their cups.\u000a\u000aHe then crossed a court paved with marble, went up the stairs and came into the guard chamber, where guards were standing in their ranks, with their muskets upon their shoulders, and snoring as loud as they could. After that he went through several rooms full of gentlemen and ladies, all asleep, some standing, others sitting. At last he came into a chamber all gilded with gold, where he saw upon a bed, the curtains of which were all open, the finest sight was ever beheld–a princess, who appeared to be about fifteen or sixteen years of age, and whose bright and, in a manner, resplendent beauty, had somewhat in it divine. He approached with trembling and admiration, and fell down before her upon his knees.\u000a\u000aAnd now, as the enchantment was at an end, the Princess awaked, and looking on him with eyes more tender than the first view might seem to admit of:\u000a\u000a“Is it you, my Prince?” said she to him. “You have waited a long while.”\u000a\u000aThe Prince, charmed with these words, and much more with the manner in which they were spoken, knew not how to show his joy and gratitude; he assured her that he loved her better than he did himself; their discourse was not well connected, they did weep more than talk– little eloquence, a great deal of love. He was more at a loss than she, and we need not wonder at it; she had time to think on what to say to him; for it is very probable (though history mentions nothing of it) that the good Fairy, during so long a sleep, had given her very agreeable dreams. In short, they talked four hours together, and yet they said not half what they had to say.\u000a\u000aIn the meanwhile all the palace awaked; everyone thought upon their particular business, and as all of them were not in love they were ready to die for hunger. The chief lady of honor, being as sharp set as other folks, grew very impatient, and told the Princess aloud that supper was served up. The Prince helped the Princess to rise; she was entirely dressed, and very magnificently, but his royal highness took care not to tell her that she was dressed like his great-grandmother, and had a point band peeping over a high collar; she looked not a bit less charming and beautiful for all that.\u000a\u000aThey went into the great hall of looking-glasses, where they supped, and were served by the Princess’s oﬀicers, the violins and hautboys played old tunes, but very excellent, though it was now above a hundred years since they had played; and after supper, without losing any\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000atime, the lord almoner married them in the chapel of the castle, and the chief lady of honor drew the curtains. They had but very little sleep–the Princess had no occasion; and the Prince left her next morning to return to the city, where his father must needs have been in pain for him. The Prince told him:\u000a\u000aThat he lost his way in the forest as he was hunting, and that he had lain in the cottage of a charcoal-burner, who gave him cheese and brown bread.\u000a\u000aThe King, his father, who was a good man, believed him; but his mother could not be persuaded it was true; and seeing that he went almost every day a-hunting, and that he always had some excuse ready for so doing, though he had lain out three or four nights together, she began to suspect that he was married, for he lived with the Princess above two whole years, and had by her two children, the eldest of which, who was a daughter, was named Morning, and the youngest, who was a son, they called Day, because he was a great deal handsomer and more beautiful than his sister.\u000a\u000aThe Queen spoke several times to her son, to inform herself after what manner he did pass his time, and that in this he ought in duty to satisfy her. But he never dared to trust her with his secret; he feared her, though he loved her, for she was of the race of the Ogres, and the King would never have married her had it not been for her vast riches; it was even whispered about the Court that she had Ogreish inclinations, and that, whenever she saw little children passing by, she had all the diﬀiculty in the world to avoid falling upon them. And so the Prince would never tell her one word.\u000a\u000aBut when the King was dead, which happened about two years afterward, and he saw himself lord and master, he openly declared his marriage; and he went in great ceremony to conduct his Queen to the palace. They made a magnificent entry into the capital city, she riding between her two children.\u000a\u000aSoon after the King went to make war with the Emperor Contalabutte, his neighbor. He left the government of the kingdom to the Queen his mother, and earnestly recommended to her care his wife and children. He was obliged to continue his expedition all the summer, and as soon as he departed the Queen-mother sent her daughter-in-law to a country house among the woods, that she might with the more ease gratify her horrible longing.\u000a\u000aSome few days afterward she went thither herself, and said to her clerk of the kitchen: “I have a mind to eat little Morning for my dinner to-morrow.” “Ah! madam,” cried the clerk of the kitchen. “I will have it so,” replied the Queen (and this she spoke in the tone of an Ogress who had a strong desire to eat fresh meat), “and will eat her with a sauce Robert.”\u000a\u000aThe poor man, knowing very well that he must not play tricks with Ogresses, took his great knife and went up into little Morning’s chamber. She was then four years old, and came up to him jumping and laughing, to take him about the neck, and ask him for some sugar-candy. Upon which he began to weep, the great knife fell out of his hand, and he went into the back yard, and killed a little lamb, and dressed it with such good sauce that his mistress assured him that she had never eaten anything so good in her life. He had at the same time taken\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000aup little Morning, and carried her to his wife, to conceal her in the lodging he had at the bottom of the courtyard.\u000a\u000aAbout eight days afterward the wicked Queen said to the clerk of the kitchen, “I will sup on little Day.”\u000a\u000aHe answered not a word, being resolved to cheat her as he had done before. He went to find out little Day, and saw him with a little foil in his hand, with which he was fencing with a great monkey, the child being then only three years of age. He took him up in his arms and carried him to his wife, that she might conceal him in her chamber along with his sister, and in the room of little Day cooked up a young kid, very tender, which the Ogress found to be wonderfully good.\u000a\u000aThis was hitherto all mighty well; but one evening this wicked Queen said to her clerk of the kitchen:\u000a\u000a“I will eat the Queen with the same sauce I had with her children.”\u000a\u000aIt was now that the poor clerk of the kitchen despaired of being able to deceive her. The young Queen was turned of twenty, not reckoning the hundred years she had been asleep; and how to find in the yard a beast so firm was what puzzled him. He took then a resolution, that he might save his own life, to cut the Queen’s throat; and going up into her chamber, with intent to do it at once, he put himself into as great fury as he could possibly, and came into the young Queen’s room with his dagger in his hand. He would not, however, surprise her, but told her, with a great deal of respect, the orders he had received from the Queen-mother.\u000a\u000a“Do it; do it” (said she, stretching out her neck). “Execute your orders, and then I shall go and see my children, my poor children, whom I so much and so tenderly loved.”\u000a\u000aFor she thought them dead ever since they had been taken away without her knowledge.\u000a\u000a“No, no, madam” (cried the poor clerk of the kitchen, all in tears); “you shall not die, and yet you shall see your children again; but then you must go home with me to my lodgings, where I have concealed them, and I shall deceive the Queen once more, by giving her in your stead a young hind.”\u000a\u000aUpon this he forthwith conducted her to his chamber, where, leaving her to embrace her children, and cry along with them, he went and dressed a young hind, which the Queen had for her supper, and devoured it with the same appetite as if it had been the young Queen. Exceedingly was she delighted with her cruelty, and she had invented a story to tell the King, at his return, how the mad wolves had eaten up the Queen his wife and her two children.\u000a\u000aOne evening, as she was, according to her custom, rambling round about the courts and yards of the palace to see if she could smell any fresh meat, she heard, in a ground room, little Day crying, for his mamma was going to whip him, because he had been naughty; and she heard, at the same time, little Morning begging pardon for her brother.\u000a\u000aThe Ogress presently knew the voice of the Queen and her children, and being quite mad that she had been thus deceived, she commanded next morning, by break of day (with a most horrible voice, which made everybody tremble), that they should bring into the middle\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000aof the great court a large tub, which she caused to be filled with toads, vipers, snakes, and all sorts of serpents, in order to have thrown into it the Queen and her children, the clerk of the kitchen, his wife and maid; all whom she had given orders should be brought thither with their hands tied behind them.\u000a\u000aThey were brought out accordingly, and the executioners were just going to throw them into the tub, when the King (who was not so soon expected) entered the court on horseback (for he came post) and asked, with the utmost astonishment, what was the meaning of that horrible spectacle.\u000a\u000aNo one dared to tell him, when the Ogress, all enraged to see what had happened, threw herself head foremost into the tub, and was instantly devoured by the ugly creatures she had ordered to be thrown into it for others. The King could not but be very sorry, for she was his mother; but he soon comforted himself with his beautiful wife and his pretty children.\u000a\u000aPublic domain. From The Blue Fairy Book (ed. Andrew Lang, 1889); Project Gutenberg ebook #503.'),'[]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'opendataloader','2ea2b06ef11390bed8085582a86d22203a8068c93eebcfb9e0b2650aece13e56',1780002789048334698,'2026-05-28 21:13:56',NULL,'2026-05-28 21:13:56','2026-05-28 21:13:57',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('67f13e4c-507e-43fe-91e6-42053bca3ed6','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','sleeping-beauty.md','Sleeping Beauty','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/sleeping-beauty.md','wiki','md',0,15,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Sleeping Beauty\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aSleeping Beauty is a classic fairy tale that revolves around a princess who is cursed to fall into a deep sleep until awakened by true love''s kiss. The story explores themes of love, fate, and the passage of time.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- **Cursed Sleep**: The central element of the tale, where the princess is put into a deep slumber by a malevolent force.\u000a- **True Love**: The awakening of the princess is contingent upon the arrival of true love, highlighting the power of love and destiny.\u000a- **Time Passage**: The narrative often includes a significant passage of time, emphasizing the inevitability of fate and the changes it brings.\u000a- **Fairy Tale Elements**: Incorporates magical beings, such as fairies, and fantastical settings that are typical of fairy tales.\u000a- **Moral Lessons**: Often conveys moral lessons about love, patience, and the consequences of actions.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe theme of Sleeping Beauty is prevalent in various cultures and adaptations, illustrating the universal appeal of love conquering adversity. It serves as a metaphor for the transformative power of love and the inevitability of fate, making it a significant narrative in the broader context of fairy tales and folklore.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf'),'["theme"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:06','2026-05-28 21:14:06',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('1d4811b2-85cb-4bc0-abee-affc1005242a','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','ogress.md','Ogress','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/ogress.md','wiki','md',0,16,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Ogress\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aAn ogress is a character type in folklore that represents a female ogre, often depicted as cruel and monstrous. She embodies themes of greed and the darker aspects of motherhood.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Female counterpart to the ogre.\u000a- Often portrayed as cruel and monstrous.\u000a- Represents themes of greed and negative maternal instincts.\u000a- Commonly found in various folklore and fairy tales.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe concept of the ogress is part of a broader tradition of mythical creatures in folklore that serve to illustrate moral lessons, societal fears, and the complexities of human nature. Ogresses often play antagonistic roles in stories, highlighting the dangers associated with unchecked desires and the darker sides of femininity.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf'),'["entity"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:11','2026-05-28 21:14:11',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('cb20b925-a06b-4056-8ff5-0bbe5fe2bab5','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','magic-and-enchantment.md','Magic and Enchantment','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/magic-and-enchantment.md','wiki','md',0,17,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Magic and Enchantment\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aMagic and enchantment are central elements in fairy tales that serve as catalysts for plot progression and character development, often embodying both benevolent and malevolent forces.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- **Supernatural Elements**: Involves spells, curses, and magical beings that influence the narrative.\u000a- **Dual Nature**: Represents both positive (benevolent) and negative (malevolent) forces that affect characters'' fates.\u000a- **Transformation**: Often leads to significant changes in characters or situations, such as physical transformations or altered realities.\u000a- **Conflict and Resolution**: Drives the central conflict and often provides the means for resolution through magical interventions.\u000a- **Symbolism**: Frequently symbolizes deeper themes such as love, fear, and the struggle between good and evil.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aMagic and enchantment are foundational themes in the broader domain of fairy tales and folklore, serving to illustrate moral lessons, cultural values, and the complexities of human nature. These elements not only enhance the fantastical aspects of stories but also reflect societal beliefs and fears, making them integral to the storytelling tradition.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf'),'["theme"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:15','2026-05-28 21:14:15',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('67e7e799-b535-47db-8f40-1c92c9a45a98','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','the-blue-fairy-book.md','The Blue Fairy Book','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/the-blue-fairy-book.md','wiki','md',0,18,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# The Blue Fairy Book\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Blue Fairy Book is a collection of fairy tales compiled by Andrew Lang, first published in 1889. It is part of Lang''s series of colored fairy books and is notable for its inclusion of classic tales such as ''The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.''\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Compiled by Andrew Lang, a prominent figure in folklore studies.\u000a- Contains a variety of fairy tales from different cultures.\u000a- Includes well-known stories, contributing to the canon of children''s literature.\u000a- Part of a series of colored fairy books, each with its own thematic focus.\u000a- Aimed at both children and adults, promoting moral lessons and cultural heritage.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe Blue Fairy Book is significant in the study of folklore and children''s literature, as it helped popularize fairy tales and made them accessible to a wider audience. It reflects the Victorian interest in folklore and the importance of storytelling in cultural education. The book has influenced subsequent adaptations and interpretations of fairy tales in various media.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf'),'["entity"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:17','2026-05-28 21:14:17',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('32454a06-9101-46e6-a8c6-0ca97f3198aa','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','the-sleeping-beauty-in-the-wood.md','The Sleeping Beauty In The Wood','/wiki/summaries/','wiki/summaries/the-sleeping-beauty-in-the-wood.md','wiki','md',0,19,'ready',NULL,unistr('# The Sleeping Beauty In The Wood\u000a\u000a**Source:** The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf | **Type:** pdf | **Pages:** 7 | **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a\u000a## Summary\u000a\u000aThe story of ''The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood'' narrates the tale of a princess cursed by an uninvited fairy to fall into a deep sleep for a hundred years, only to be awakened by a prince. The narrative explores themes of love, fate, and the consequences of envy, culminating in the eventual triumph of love over adversity. The tale also includes elements of magic, such as fairies and enchanted objects, and addresses the darker aspects of human nature through the character of the ogress queen.\u000a\u000a## Related Concepts\u000a\u000a- [Sleeping Beauty](../concepts/sleeping-beauty.md)\u000a- [Fairy Tales](../concepts/fairy-tales.md)\u000a- [Ogress](../concepts/ogress.md)\u000a- [Magic and Enchantment](../concepts/magic-and-enchantment.md)\u000a- [The Blue Fairy Book](../concepts/the-blue-fairy-book.md)\u000a\u000a## Source Information\u000a\u000a- **File:** The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf\u000a- **Type:** pdf\u000a- **Pages:** 7\u000a- **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a- **Parser:** opendataloader\u000a\u000a[^1]: The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf\u000a'),'[]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:17','2026-05-28 21:14:17','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e');
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf','Snow White And The Seven Dwarfs','sources/','sources/Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf','source','pdf',1285593,20,'ready',6,unistr('![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile1.png)\u000a\u000a# Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs\u000a\u000a14 15\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a## Part One\u000a\u000aOnce upon a time there was a little princess… She was called Snow White because she was white as snow. Her lips were red as blood. And her hair was black as night. She was a beautiful girl, sweet and gentle. She lived in a palace with her father and her stepmother: the King and the Queen. The Queen was a beautiful woman. But she was wicked and vain.\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile2.png)\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile3.png)\u000a\u000a*Every day, the Queen looked in her magic mirror and asked:*\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000aAnd every day, the mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“You, my Queen! You are! You are the most beautiful of all.”\u000a\u000aTime passed and little Snow White became a beautiful young lady. Everybody loved her. One morning, when the Queen asked her magic mirror:\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000a16 17\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile4.png)\u000a\u000aThe mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“My Queen, you are beautiful, it’s true! But Snow White is more beautiful than you.”\u000a\u000aThe Queen was furious! She planned a horrible crime. She called a huntsman and commanded him: “Take Snow White deep into the forest… and kill her!” “But… Your Majesty! The beloved Princess!” he said. “Kill Snow White! And take out her heart,” the Queen commanded again.\u000a\u000a## Part Two\u000a\u000aThe huntsman took Snow White deep into the woods. “Forgive me, my Princess… I have orders from the Queen to kill you! But I can’t do that!” he said. “Oh, good man!” she cried, “I’ll run into the forest and I’ll never come back!” Snow White ran and ran through the dark forest. She was so scared! Then, she found a little house. The door was open. She went in.\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile5.png)\u000a\u000a18 19\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile6.png)\u000a\u000aIn the morning, she woke up and saw the seven masters of the house looking at her. They were seven dwarfs. “Who are you?” they asked. “My name is Snow White,” she answered. And she told them the whole story about the wicked Queen. “Princess Snow White, you can stay,” the Dwarfs said. “Can you help us cook and clean?” “Yes! I can cook and I can wash. And I can make beds,” she said happily. “Please, remember: don’t open the door to strangers!” said the Dwarfs. “The Queen will find you.”\u000a\u000a*A little table was set for dinner: seven little chairs, seven little bowls, seven little spoons, seven little knives, seven little forks, and seven little mugs. Snow White was hungry and thirsty. She thought: “I’ll have a little bread from each bowl. And I’ll drink a little wine from each mug.” Then, feeling so tired, she lay down on one of the seven little beds. And she fell asleep.*\u000a\u000a20 21\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a## Part Three\u000a\u000aAt the Palace, the Queen asked her magic mirror:\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000aThe mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“My Queen, you are beautiful, it’s true! But Snow White is more beautiful than you. In the mountains, she dwells And she is still alive and well.”\u000a\u000aThe Queen was absolutely furious! “I will kill Snow White myself!” she thought. She made a deadly poison. Then, she put the poison in a beautiful red apple.\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile7.png)\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile8.png)\u000a\u000aThe Queen, dressed in old clothes, went to the little house in the mountains. She knocked on the door, calling: “APPLES! JUICY APPLES! APPLES FOR SALE!” Snow White answered: “I’m sorry, madam. I can’t open the door to strangers.” “Oh, I’m just an old woman! Open the window and take one apple,” said the Queen. Snow White opened the window, took the apple and had one bite. She fell dead to the floor! “Now, I’m the most beautiful of all. Ha, ha, ha!” the Queen said and she ran away.\u000a\u000a23\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile9.png)\u000a\u000aBack at the Palace, the Queen ran to her magic mirror:\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000aThe mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“You, my Queen! You are! You are the most beautiful of all.”\u000a\u000aThe wicked Queen was happy now because the magic mirror never lied.\u000a\u000a## Part Four\u000a\u000aThe Seven Dwarfs found Snow White dead on the floor. In her honor, they made her a glass coffin and placed it on top of a mountain. One day, a young Prince stopped to see her. And he fell in love with Snow White. With a kiss, she came back to a new life with her Prince. Snow White and the Prince had a big wedding. Everyone was invited to the celebration. Even the wicked Queen! And that night, when the Queen saw Snow White, she choked with envy and hate. And she fell dead to the floor. The Prince and the Princess lived happily ever after…\u000a\u000a### THE END\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile10.png)\u000a\u000a24 25'),'[]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'opendataloader','641632581ed075497cc96fefcee2fde1fdcd2407df2004d3dd252a312375179e',1780002789049924775,'2026-05-28 21:14:17',NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:17','2026-05-28 21:14:20',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('f48ff4bf-1ea5-4876-af44-08f956c8a4ea','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','snow-white.md','Snow White','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/snow-white.md','wiki','md',0,21,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Snow White\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aSnow White is a central character in a classic fairy tale known for her beauty and kindness. Her story has been adapted into various forms of literature and film, making her an iconic figure in popular culture.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Renowned for her exceptional beauty.\u000a- Embodies kindness and innocence.\u000a- Central figure in the tale involving her escape from a wicked queen.\u000a- Associated with seven dwarfs who aid her in her journey.\u000a- Represents themes of jealousy, friendship, and the triumph of good over evil.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aSnow White''s narrative is part of the broader tradition of fairy tales that explore moral lessons and human virtues. Her story has influenced countless adaptations in literature, film, and other media, solidifying her status as a timeless character in storytelling.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),'["entity"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:28','2026-05-28 21:14:28',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('2f85d8b5-9aeb-4316-ac0c-576dfe4934ba','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','the-seven-dwarfs.md','The Seven Dwarfs','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/the-seven-dwarfs.md','wiki','md',0,22,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# The Seven Dwarfs\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Seven Dwarfs are fictional characters from the fairy tale "Snow White," known for their small stature and distinct personalities. They serve as loyal protectors of Snow White, embodying themes of friendship and loyalty.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Each dwarf has a unique name and personality, often reflecting their traits (e.g., Doc, Grumpy, Happy).\u000a- They live together in a cottage in the woods, symbolizing camaraderie and teamwork.\u000a- The dwarfs are characterized by their dedication to Snow White, showcasing their protective nature.\u000a- They play a crucial role in the narrative by defending Snow White from the evil Queen.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe Seven Dwarfs are integral to the story of "Snow White," a classic fairy tale that explores themes of good versus evil, innocence, and the importance of friendship. Their role highlights the significance of loyalty and support in overcoming adversity, making them memorable figures in popular culture and adaptations of the tale.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),'["entity"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:33','2026-05-28 21:14:33',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('79c1f417-c3a5-4ee4-a2b7-1b8009f70bbc','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','the-wicked-queen.md','The Wicked Queen','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/the-wicked-queen.md','wiki','md',0,23,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# The Wicked Queen\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Wicked Queen is the primary antagonist in the fairy tale "Snow White," representing the darker aspects of human nature, particularly vanity and jealousy. Her obsession with being the fairest in the land drives her to commit heinous acts against her stepdaughter, Snow White.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Embodies themes of vanity and jealousy.\u000a- Utilizes dark magic and deception to achieve her goals.\u000a- Portrayed as a beautiful yet malevolent figure.\u000a- Ultimately meets her demise due to her own malicious actions.\u000a- Represents the archetype of the evil stepmother in fairy tales.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe Wicked Queen serves as a cautionary figure within the broader context of fairy tales, illustrating the consequences of unchecked vanity and jealousy. Her character highlights the moral lessons often found in folklore, where good ultimately triumphs over evil, reinforcing the values of kindness and humility.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),'["entity"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:36','2026-05-28 21:14:36',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('fe2acff7-ce61-4314-ab82-08e81663c4ad','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','magic-mirror.md','Magic Mirror','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/magic-mirror.md','wiki','md',0,24,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [instrument]\u000asources: [Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Magic Mirror\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Magic Mirror is a mystical instrument featured in the fairy tale "Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs," serving as a source of truth and revelation for the Queen regarding her beauty and status.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Provides the Queen with information about her beauty relative to others.\u000a- Acts as a catalyst for the Queen''s jealousy and actions against Snow White.\u000a- Functions as a symbol of vanity and the quest for superiority.\u000a- Reflects the inner desires and insecurities of the characters involved.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aIn the broader context of fairy tales, the Magic Mirror represents the theme of self-perception and the dangers of vanity. It highlights the consequences of obsession with beauty and the lengths one may go to maintain a sense of superiority, thus driving the narrative of "Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs."\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),'["instrument"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:38','2026-05-28 21:14:38',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('2f33fdde-9e54-414d-91cf-eb91dd404580','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','poisoned-apple.md','Poisoned Apple','/wiki/concepts/','wiki/concepts/poisoned-apple.md','wiki','md',0,25,'ready',NULL,unistr('---\u000atags: [instrument]\u000asources: [Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Poisoned Apple\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Poisoned Apple is a pivotal plot device in the fairy tale "Snow White," representing the deceit and treachery of the Evil Queen who uses it in her attempt to eliminate Snow White.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Symbolizes deceit and betrayal.\u000a- Serves as a catalyst for the story''s conflict.\u000a- Represents the lengths to which the Queen will go to achieve her goals.\u000a- Acts as a physical manifestation of the Queen''s malice.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe Poisoned Apple is integral to the narrative of "Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs," illustrating themes of jealousy, the struggle between good and evil, and the consequences of vanity. It highlights the dangers of trust and the impact of malicious intentions within fairy tales.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),'["instrument"]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:40','2026-05-28 21:14:40',NULL);
INSERT INTO documents VALUES('636ec000-a462-48da-97d2-4441e9e4c468','9cd1d612-8e71-4b4f-a3b4-78ed5ca39291','snow-white-and-the-seven-dwarfs.md','Snow White And The Seven Dwarfs','/wiki/summaries/','wiki/summaries/snow-white-and-the-seven-dwarfs.md','wiki','md',0,26,'ready',NULL,unistr('# Snow White And The Seven Dwarfs\u000a\u000a**Source:** Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf | **Type:** pdf | **Pages:** 6 | **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a\u000a## Summary\u000a\u000aThe story of Snow White follows a beautiful princess who is envied by her wicked stepmother, the Queen. After the Queen orders her death, Snow White escapes into the forest and finds refuge with seven dwarfs. Ultimately, the Queen''s jealousy leads to her downfall, while Snow White is revived by a prince''s kiss, leading to their happy ending.\u000a\u000a## Related Concepts\u000a\u000a- [Snow White](../concepts/snow-white.md)\u000a- [The Seven Dwarfs](../concepts/the-seven-dwarfs.md)\u000a- [The Wicked Queen](../concepts/the-wicked-queen.md)\u000a- [Magic Mirror](../concepts/magic-mirror.md)\u000a- [Poisoned Apple](../concepts/poisoned-apple.md)\u000a\u000a## Source Information\u000a\u000a- **File:** Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf\u000a- **Type:** pdf\u000a- **Pages:** 6\u000a- **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a- **Parser:** opendataloader\u000a\u000a[^1]: Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf\u000a'),'[]',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-28 21:14:40','2026-05-28 21:14:40','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347');
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('d7e32d6b-8255-4cac-b451-f274a8e0b8d5','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',1,unistr('# Cinderella, or the Little Glass Slipper\u000a\u000aOnce there was a gentleman who married, for his second wife, the proudest and most haughty woman that was ever seen. She had, by a former husband, two daughters of her own humor, who were, indeed, exactly like her in all things. He had likewise, by another wife, a young daughter, but of unparalleled goodness and sweetness of temper, which she took from her mother, who was the best creature in the world.\u000a\u000aNo sooner were the ceremonies of the wedding over but the mother-in-law began to show herself in her true colors. She could not bear the good qualities of this pretty girl, and the less because they made her own daughters appear the more odious. She employed her in the meanest work of the house: she scoured the dishes, tables, etc., and scrubbed madam’s chamber, and those of misses, her daughters; she lay up in a sorry garret, upon a wretched straw bed, while her sisters lay in fine rooms, with floors all inlaid, upon beds of the very newest fashion, and where they had looking-glasses so large that they might see themselves at their full length from head to foot.\u000a\u000aThe poor girl bore all patiently, and dared not tell her father, who would have rattled her off; for his wife governed him entirely. When she had done her work, she used to go into the chimney-corner, and sit down among cinders and ashes, which made her commonly be called Cinderwench; but the youngest, who was not so rude and uncivil as the eldest, called her Cinderella. However, Cinderella, notwithstanding her mean apparel, was a hundred times handsomer than her sisters, though they were always dressed very richly.\u000a\u000aIt happened that the King’s son gave a ball, and invited all persons of fashion to it. Our young misses were also invited, for they cut a very grand figure among the quality. They were mightily delighted at this invitation, and wonderfully busy in choosing out such gowns, petticoats, and head-clothes as might become them. This was a new trouble to Cinderella; for it was she who ironed her sisters’ linen, and plaited their ruffles; they talked all day long of nothing but how they should be dressed.\u000a\u000a“For my part,” said the eldest, “I will wear my red velvet suit with French trimming.”\u000a\u000a“And I,” said the youngest, “shall have my usual petticoat; but then, to make amends for that, I will put on my gold-flowered manteau, and my diamond stomacher, which is far from being the most ordinary one in the world.”\u000a\u000aThey sent for the best tire-woman they could get to make up their head-dresses and adjust their double pinners, and they had their red brushes and patches from Mademoiselle de la Poche.'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('9292ee83-a6e2-4007-9372-211027590cae','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',2,unistr('Cinderella was likewise called up to them to be consulted in all these matters, for she had excellent notions, and advised them always for the best, nay, and offered her services to dress their heads, which they were very willing she should do. As she was doing this, they said to her:\u000a\u000a“Cinderella, would you not be glad to go to the ball?” “Alas!” said she, “you only jeer me; it is not for such as I am to go thither.” “Thou art in the right of it,” replied they; “it would make the people laugh to see a Cinderwench at a ball.”\u000a\u000aAnyone but Cinderella would have dressed their heads awry, but she was very good, and dressed them perfectly well They were almost two days without eating, so much were they transported with joy. They broke above a dozen laces in trying to be laced up close, that they might have a fine slender shape, and they were continually at their looking-glass. At last the happy day came; they went to Court, and Cinderella followed them with her eyes as long as she could, and when she had lost sight of them, she fell a-crying.\u000a\u000aHer godmother, who saw her all in tears, asked her what was the matter. “I wish I could–I wish I could–“; she was not able to speak the rest, being interrupted by her tears and sobbing. This godmother of hers, who was a fairy, said to her, “Thou wishest thou couldst go to the ball; is it not so?” “Y–es,” cried Cinderella, with a great sigh.\u000a\u000a“Well,” said her godmother, “be but a good girl, and I will contrive that thou shalt go.” Then she took her into her chamber, and said to her, “Run into the garden, and bring me a pumpkin.”\u000a\u000aCinderella went immediately to gather the finest she could get, and brought it to her godmother, not being able to imagine how this pumpkin could make her go to the ball. Her godmother scooped out all the inside of it, having left nothing but the rind; which done, she struck it with her wand, and the pumpkin was instantly turned into a fine coach, gilded all over with gold.\u000a\u000aShe then went to look into her mouse-trap, where she found six mice, all alive, and ordered Cinderella to lift up a little the trapdoor, when, giving each mouse, as it went out, a little tap with her wand, the mouse was that moment turned into a fine horse, which altogether made a very fine set of six horses of a beautiful mouse-colored dapple-gray. Being at a loss for a coachman,\u000a\u000a“I will go and see,” says Cinderella, “if there is never a rat in the rat-trap–we may make a coachman of him.”\u000a\u000a“Thou art in the right,” replied her godmother; “go and look.”\u000a\u000aCinderella brought the trap to her, and in it there were three huge rats. The fairy made choice of one of the three which had the largest beard, and, having touched him with her'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('db81978e-8814-4d2f-99dd-589d83adb128','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',3,unistr('wand, he was turned into a fat, jolly coachman, who had the smartest whiskers eyes ever beheld. After that, she said to her:\u000a\u000a“Go again into the garden, and you will find six lizards behind the watering-pot, bring them to me.”\u000a\u000aShe had no sooner done so but her godmother turned them into six footmen, who skipped up immediately behind the coach, with their liveries all bedaubed with gold and silver, and clung as close behind each other as if they had done nothing else their whole lives. The Fairy then said to Cinderella:\u000a\u000a“Well, you see here an equipage fit to go to the ball with; are you not pleased with it?” “Oh! yes,” cried she; “but must I go thither as I am, in these nasty rags?”\u000a\u000aHer godmother only just touched her with her wand, and, at the same instant, her clothes were turned into cloth of gold and silver, all beset with jewels. This done, she gave her a pair of glass slippers, the prettiest in the whole world. Being thus decked out, she got up into her coach; but her godmother, above all things, commanded her not to stay till after midnight, telling her, at the same time, that if she stayed one moment longer, the coach would be a pumpkin again, her horses mice, her coachman a rat, her footmen lizards, and her clothes become just as they were before.\u000a\u000aShe promised her godmother she would not fail of leaving the ball before midnight; and then away she drives, scarce able to contain herself for joy. The King’s son who was told that a great princess, whom nobody knew, was come, ran out to receive her; he gave her his hand\u000a\u000a- as she alighted out of the coach, and led her into the ball, among all the company. There was immediately a profound silence, they left off dancing, and the violins ceased to play, so attentive was everyone to contemplate the singular beauties of the unknown new-comer. Nothing was then heard but a confused noise of: “Ha! how handsome she is! Ha! how handsome she is!”\u000a\u000aThe King himself, old as he was, could not help watching her, and telling the Queen softly that it was a long time since he had seen so beautiful and lovely a creature.\u000a\u000aAll the ladies were busied in considering her clothes and headdress, that they might have some made next day after the same pattern, provided they could meet with such fine material and as able hands to make them.\u000a\u000aThe King’s son conducted her to the most honorable seat, and afterward took her out to dance with him; she danced so very gracefully that they all more and more admired her. A fine collation was served up, whereof the young prince ate not a morsel, so intently was he busied in gazing on her.\u000a\u000aShe went and sat down by her sisters, showing them a thousand civilities, giving them part of the oranges and citrons which the Prince had presented her with, which very much surprised them, for they did not know her. While Cinderella was thus amusing her sisters, she heard the clock strike eleven and three-quarters, whereupon she immediately made a courtesy to the company and hasted away as fast as she could.'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('bc3adbde-ae18-4c53-8b6e-6654d2b867b7','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',4,unistr('When she got home she ran to seek out her godmother, and, after having thanked her, she said she could not but heartily wish she might go next day to the ball, because the King’s son had desired her.\u000a\u000aAs she was eagerly telling her godmother whatever had passed at the ball, her two sisters knocked at the door, which Cinderella ran and opened.\u000a\u000a“How long you have stayed!” cried she, gaping, rubbing her eyes and stretching herself as if she had been just waked out of her sleep; she had not, however, any manner of inclination to sleep since they went from home.\u000a\u000a“If thou hadst been at the ball,” said one of her sisters, “thou wouldst not have been tired with it. There came thither the finest princess, the most beautiful ever was seen with mortal eyes; she showed us a thousand civilities, and gave us oranges and citrons.”\u000a\u000aCinderella seemed very indifferent in the matter; indeed, she asked them the name of that princess; but they told her they did not know it, and that the King’s son was very uneasy on her account and would give all the world to know who she was. At this Cinderella, smiling, replied:\u000a\u000a“She must, then, be very beautiful indeed; how happy you have been! Could not I see her? Ah! dear Miss Charlotte, do lend me your yellow suit of clothes which you wear every day.” “Ay, to be sure!” cried Miss Charlotte; “lend my clothes to such a dirty Cinderwench as thou art! I should be a fool.” Cinderella, indeed, expected well such answer, and was very glad of the refusal; for she would have been sadly put to it if her sister had lent her what she asked for jestingly.\u000a\u000aThe next day the two sisters were at the ball, and so was Cinderella, but dressed more magnificently than before. The King’s son was always by her, and never ceased his compliments and kind speeches to her; to whom all this was so far from being tiresome that she quite forgot what her godmother had recommended to her; so that she, at last, counted the clock striking twelve when she took it to be no more than eleven; she then rose up and fled, as nimble as a deer. The Prince followed, but could not overtake her. She left behind one of her glass slippers, which the Prince took up most carefully. She got home but quite out of breath, and in her nasty old clothes, having nothing left her of all her finery but one of the little slippers, fellow to that she dropped. The guards at the palace gate were asked:\u000a\u000aIf they had not seen a princess go out. Who said: They had seen nobody go out but a young girl, very meanly dressed, and who had more the air of a poor country wench than a gentlewoman. When the two sisters returned from the ball Cinderella asked them: If they had been well diverted, and if the fine lady had been there.\u000a\u000aThey told her: Yes, but that she hurried away immediately when it struck twelve, and with so much haste that she dropped one of her little glass slippers, the prettiest in the world, which the King’s son had taken up; that he had done nothing but look at her all the time'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('0d339407-23d4-4d91-9c43-e5047e168521','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',5,unistr('- at the ball, and that most certainly he was very much in love with the beautiful person who owned the glass slipper.\u000a\u000aWhat they said was very true; for a few days after the King’s son caused it to be proclaimed, by sound of trumpet, that he would marry her whose foot the slipper would just fit. They whom he employed began to try it upon the princesses, then the duchesses and all the Court, but in vain; it was brought to the two sisters, who did all they possibly could to thrust their foot into the slipper, but they could not effect it. Cinderella, who saw all this, and knew her slipper, said to them, laughing:\u000a\u000a“Let me see if it will not fit me.” Her sisters burst out a-laughing, and began to banter her. The gentleman who was sent to try the slipper looked earnestly at Cinderella, and, finding her very handsome, said: It was but just that she should try, and that he had orders to let everyone make trial.\u000a\u000aHe obliged Cinderella to sit down, and, putting the slipper to her foot, he found it went on very easily, and fitted her as if it had been made of wax. The astonishment her two sisters were in was excessively great, but still abundantly greater when Cinderella pulled out of her pocket the other slipper, and put it on her foot. Thereupon, in came her godmother, who, having touched with her wand Cinderella’s clothes, made them richer and more magnificent than any of those she had before.\u000a\u000aAnd now her two sisters found her to be that fine, beautiful lady whom they had seen at the ball. They threw themselves at her feet to beg pardon for all the ill-treatment they had made her undergo. Cinderella took them up, and, as she embraced them, cried:\u000a\u000aThat she forgave them with all her heart, and desired them always to love her.\u000a\u000aShe was conducted to the young prince, dressed as she was; he thought her more charming than ever, and, a few days after, married her. Cinderella, who was no less good than beautiful, gave her two sisters lodgings in the palace, and that very same day matched them with two great lords of the Court.(1)\u000a\u000a(1) Charles Perrault.\u000a\u000aPublic domain. From The Blue Fairy Book (ed. Andrew Lang, 1889); Project Gutenberg ebook #503.'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('f67d8312-3a27-4449-9bc5-65980ff5f636','fd3ae084-fbd2-4de8-a63a-4c671574d943',1,unistr('# Little Red Riding Hood\u000a\u000aOnce upon a time there lived in a certain village a little country girl, the prettiest creature was ever seen. Her mother was excessively fond of her; and her grandmother doted on her still more. This good woman had made for her a little red riding-hood; which became the girl so extremely well that everybody called her Little Red Riding-Hood.\u000a\u000aOne day her mother, having made some custards, said to her: “Go, my dear, and see how thy grandmamma does, for I hear she has been very ill; carry her a custard, and this little pot of butter.” Little Red Riding-Hood set out immediately to go to her grandmother, who lived in another village.\u000a\u000aAs she was going through the wood, she met with Gaffer Wolf, who had a very great mind to eat her up, but he dared not, because of some faggot-makers hard by in the forest. He asked her whither she was going. The poor child, who did not know that it was dangerous to stay and hear a wolf talk, said to him:\u000a\u000a“I am going to see my grandmamma and carry her a custard and a little pot of butter from my mamma.”\u000a\u000a“Does she live far off?” said the Wolf. “Oh! ay,” answered Little Red Riding-Hood; “it is beyond that mill you see there, at the first house in the village.” “Well,” said the Wolf, “and I’ll go and see her too. I’ll go this way and you go that, and we shall see who will be there soonest.”\u000a\u000aThe Wolf began to run as fast as he could, taking the nearest way, and the little girl went by that farthest about, diverting herself in gathering nuts, running after butterflies, and making nosegays of such little flowers as she met with. The Wolf was not long before he got to the old woman’s house. He knocked at the door–tap, tap.\u000a\u000a“Who’s there?” “Your grandchild, Little Red Riding-Hood,” replied the Wolf, counterfeiting her voice; “who has brought you a custard and a little pot of butter sent you by mamma.” The good grandmother, who was in bed, because she was somewhat ill, cried out:'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('ce39b92e-fdfa-4772-a071-88bf3370eead','fd3ae084-fbd2-4de8-a63a-4c671574d943',2,unistr('“Pull the bobbin, and the latch will go up.”\u000a\u000aThe Wolf pulled the bobbin, and the door opened, and then presently he fell upon the good woman and ate her up in a moment, for it was above three days that he had not touched a bit. He then shut the door and went into the grandmother’s bed, expecting Little Red Riding-Hood, who came some time afterward and knocked at the door–tap, tap.\u000a\u000a“Who’s there?” Little Red Riding-Hood, hearing the big voice of the Wolf, was at first afraid; but believing her grandmother had got a cold and was hoarse, answered: “‘Tis your grandchild, Little Red Riding-Hood, who has brought you a custard and a little pot of butter mamma sends you.” The Wolf cried out to her, softening his voice as much as he could: “Pull the bobbin, and the latch will go up.” Little Red Riding-Hood pulled the bobbin, and the door opened. The Wolf, seeing her come in, said to her, hiding himself under the bed-clothes: “Put the custard and the little pot of butter upon the stool, and come and lie down with me.” Little Red Riding-Hood undressed herself and went into bed, where, being greatly amazed to see how her grandmother looked in her night-clothes, she said to her: “Grandmamma, what great arms you have got!” “That is the better to hug thee, my dear.” “Grandmamma, what great legs you have got!” “That is to run the better, my child.” “Grandmamma, what great ears you have got!” “That is to hear the better, my child.” “Grandmamma, what great eyes you have got!” “It is to see the better, my child.” “Grandmamma, what great teeth you have got!” “That is to eat thee up.” And, saying these words, this wicked wolf fell upon Little Red Riding-Hood, and ate her all up.\u000a\u000aPublic domain. From The Blue Fairy Book (ed. Andrew Lang, 1889); Project Gutenberg ebook #503.'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('f75696a2-bedc-413b-aef9-f0ac29c9fc1b','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',1,unistr('# The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood\u000a\u000aThere were formerly a king and a queen, who were so sorry that they had no children; so sorry that it cannot be expressed. They went to all the waters in the world; vows, pilgrimages, all ways were tried, and all to no purpose.\u000a\u000aAt last, however, the Queen had a daughter. There was a very fine christening; and the Princess had for her god-mothers all the fairies they could find in the whole kingdom (they found seven), that every one of them might give her a gift, as was the custom of fairies in those days. By this means the Princess had all the perfections imaginable.\u000a\u000aAfter the ceremonies of the christening were over, all the company returned to the King’s palace, where was prepared a great feast for the fairies. There was placed before every one of them a magnificent cover with a case of massive gold, wherein were a spoon, knife, and fork, all of pure gold set with diamonds and rubies. But as they were all sitting down at table they saw come into the hall a very old fairy, whom they had not invited, because it was above fifty years since she had been out of a certain tower, and she was believed to be either dead or enchanted.\u000a\u000aThe King ordered her a cover, but could not furnish her with a case of gold as the others, because they had only seven made for the seven fairies. The old Fairy fancied she was slighted, and muttered some threats between her teeth. One of the young fairies who sat by her overheard how she grumbled; and, judging that she might give the little Princess some unlucky gift, went, as soon as they rose from table, and hid herself behind the hangings, that she might speak last, and repair, as much as she could, the evil which the old Fairy might intend.\u000a\u000aIn the meanwhile all the fairies began to give their gifts to the Princess. The youngest gave her for gift that she should be the most beautiful person in the world; the next, that she should have the wit of an angel; the third, that she should have a wonderful grace in everything she did; the fourth, that she should dance perfectly well; the fifth, that she should sing like a nightingale; and the sixth, that she should play all kinds of music to the utmost perfection.\u000a\u000aThe old Fairy’s turn coming next, with a head shaking more with spite than age, she said that the Princess should have her hand pierced with a spindle and die of the wound. This terrible gift made the whole company tremble, and everybody fell a-crying.\u000a\u000aAt this very instant the young Fairy came out from behind the hangings, and spake these words aloud:'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('175ed73f-7991-46ca-98bc-7547771b99f8','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',2,unistr('“Assure yourselves, O King and Queen, that your daughter shall not die of this disaster. It is true, I have no power to undo entirely what my elder has done. The Princess shall indeed pierce her hand with a spindle; but, instead of dying, she shall only fall into a profound sleep, which shall last a hundred years, at the expiration of which a king’s son shall come and awake her.”\u000a\u000aThe King, to avoid the misfortune foretold by the old Fairy, caused immediately proclamation to be made, whereby everybody was forbidden, on pain of death, to spin with a distaff and spindle, or to have so much as any spindle in their houses. About fifteen or sixteen years after, the King and Queen being gone to one of their houses of pleasure, the young Princess happened one day to divert herself in running up and down the palace; when going up from one apartment to another, she came into a little room on the top of the tower, where a good old woman, alone, was spinning with her spindle. This good woman had never heard of the King’s proclamation against spindles.\u000a\u000a“What are you doing there, goody?” said the Princess. “I am spinning, my pretty child,” said the old woman, who did not know who she was. “Ha!” said the Princess, “this is very pretty; how do you do it? Give it to me, that I may see if I can do so.”\u000a\u000aShe had no sooner taken it into her hand than, whether being very hasty at it, somewhat unhandy, or that the decree of the Fairy had so ordained it, it ran into her hand, and she fell down in a swoon.\u000a\u000aThe good old woman, not knowing very well what to do in this affair, cried out for help. People came in from every quarter in great numbers; they threw water upon the Princess’s face, unlaced her, struck her on the palms of her hands, and rubbed her temples with Hungarywater; but nothing would bring her to herself.\u000a\u000aAnd now the King, who came up at the noise, bethought himself of the prediction of the fairies, and, judging very well that this must necessarily come to pass, since the fairies had said it, caused the Princess to be carried into the finest apartment in his palace, and to be laid upon a bed all embroidered with gold and silver.\u000a\u000aOne would have taken her for a little angel, she was so very beautiful; for her swooning away had not diminished one bit of her complexion; her cheeks were carnation, and her lips were coral; indeed, her eyes were shut, but she was heard to breathe softly, which satisfied those about her that she was not dead. The King commanded that they should not disturb her, but let her sleep quietly till her hour of awaking was come.\u000a\u000aThe good Fairy who had saved her life by condemning her to sleep a hundred years was in the kingdom of Matakin, twelve thousand leagues off, when this accident befell the Princess; but she was instantly informed of it by a little dwarf, who had boots of seven leagues, that is, boots with which he could tread over seven leagues of ground in one stride. The Fairy came away immediately, and she arrived, about an hour after, in a fiery chariot drawn by dragons.\u000a\u000aThe King handed her out of the chariot, and she approved everything he had done, but as she had very great foresight, she thought when the Princess should awake she might not know'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('e7e60dc9-65fa-42a3-9864-8a02409069e1','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',3,unistr('what to do with herself, being all alone in this old palace; and this was what she did: she touched with her wand everything in the palace (except the King and Queen)–governesses, maids of honor, ladies of the bedchamber, gentlemen, oﬀicers, stewards, cooks, undercooks, scullions, guards, with their beefeaters, pages, footmen; she likewise touched all the horses which were in the stables, pads as well as others, the great dogs in the outward court and pretty little Mopsey too, the Princess’s little spaniel, which lay by her on the bed.\u000a\u000aImmediately upon her touching them they all fell asleep, that they might not awake before their mistress and that they might be ready to wait upon her when she wanted them. The very spits at the fire, as full as they could hold of partridges and pheasants, did fall asleep also. All this was done in a moment. Fairies are not long in doing their business.\u000a\u000aAnd now the King and the Queen, having kissed their dear child without waking her, went out of the palace and put forth a proclamation that nobody should dare to come near it.\u000a\u000aThis, however, was not necessary, for in a quarter of an hour’s time there grew up all round about the park such a vast number of trees, great and small, bushes and brambles, twining one within another, that neither man nor beast could pass through; so that nothing could be seen but the very top of the towers of the palace; and that, too, not unless it was a good way off. Nobody; doubted but the Fairy gave herein a very extraordinary sample of her art, that the Princess, while she continued sleeping, might have nothing to fear from any curious people.\u000a\u000aWhen a hundred years were gone and passed the son of the King then reigning, and who was of another family from that of the sleeping Princess, being gone a-hunting on that side of the country, asked:\u000a\u000aWhat those towers were which he saw in the middle of a great thick wood? Everyone answered according as they had heard. Some said: That it was a ruinous old castle, haunted by spirits. Others, That all the sorcerers and witches of the country kept there their sabbath or night’s meeting.\u000a\u000aThe common opinion was: That an ogre lived there, and that he carried thither all the little children he could catch, that he might eat them up at his leisure, without anybody being able to follow him, as having himself only the power to pass through the wood.\u000a\u000aThe Prince was at a stand, not knowing what to believe, when a very good countryman spake to him thus:\u000a\u000a“May it please your royal highness, it is now about fifty years since I heard from my father, who heard my grandfather say, that there was then in this castle a princess, the most beautiful was ever seen; that she must sleep there a hundred years, and should be waked by a king’s son, for whom she was reserved.”\u000a\u000aThe young Prince was all on fire at these words, believing, without weighing the matter, that he could put an end to this rare adventure; and, pushed on by love and honor, resolved that moment to look into it.'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('b15f555d-ebb8-4113-8cbf-4597c46d2fab','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',4,unistr('Scarce had he advanced toward the wood when all the great trees, the bushes, and brambles gave way of themselves to let him pass through; he walked up to the castle which he saw at the end of a large avenue which he went into; and what a little surprised him was that he saw none of his people could follow him, because the trees closed again as soon as he had passed through them. However, he did not cease from continuing his way; a young and amorous prince is always valiant.\u000a\u000aHe came into a spacious outward court, where everything he saw might have frozen the most fearless person with horror. There reigned all over a most frightful silence; the image of death everywhere showed itself, and there was nothing to be seen but stretched-out bodies of men and animals, all seeming to be dead. He, however, very well knew, by the ruby faces and pimpled noses of the beefeaters, that they were only asleep; and their goblets, wherein still remained some drops of wine, showed plainly that they fell asleep in their cups.\u000a\u000aHe then crossed a court paved with marble, went up the stairs and came into the guard chamber, where guards were standing in their ranks, with their muskets upon their shoulders, and snoring as loud as they could. After that he went through several rooms full of gentlemen and ladies, all asleep, some standing, others sitting. At last he came into a chamber all gilded with gold, where he saw upon a bed, the curtains of which were all open, the finest sight was ever beheld–a princess, who appeared to be about fifteen or sixteen years of age, and whose bright and, in a manner, resplendent beauty, had somewhat in it divine. He approached with trembling and admiration, and fell down before her upon his knees.\u000a\u000aAnd now, as the enchantment was at an end, the Princess awaked, and looking on him with eyes more tender than the first view might seem to admit of:\u000a\u000a“Is it you, my Prince?” said she to him. “You have waited a long while.”\u000a\u000aThe Prince, charmed with these words, and much more with the manner in which they were spoken, knew not how to show his joy and gratitude; he assured her that he loved her better than he did himself; their discourse was not well connected, they did weep more than talk– little eloquence, a great deal of love. He was more at a loss than she, and we need not wonder at it; she had time to think on what to say to him; for it is very probable (though history mentions nothing of it) that the good Fairy, during so long a sleep, had given her very agreeable dreams. In short, they talked four hours together, and yet they said not half what they had to say.\u000a\u000aIn the meanwhile all the palace awaked; everyone thought upon their particular business, and as all of them were not in love they were ready to die for hunger. The chief lady of honor, being as sharp set as other folks, grew very impatient, and told the Princess aloud that supper was served up. The Prince helped the Princess to rise; she was entirely dressed, and very magnificently, but his royal highness took care not to tell her that she was dressed like his great-grandmother, and had a point band peeping over a high collar; she looked not a bit less charming and beautiful for all that.\u000a\u000aThey went into the great hall of looking-glasses, where they supped, and were served by the Princess’s oﬀicers, the violins and hautboys played old tunes, but very excellent, though it was now above a hundred years since they had played; and after supper, without losing any'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('ec1639b5-0193-4755-bb40-d4c8eb987abb','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',5,unistr('time, the lord almoner married them in the chapel of the castle, and the chief lady of honor drew the curtains. They had but very little sleep–the Princess had no occasion; and the Prince left her next morning to return to the city, where his father must needs have been in pain for him. The Prince told him:\u000a\u000aThat he lost his way in the forest as he was hunting, and that he had lain in the cottage of a charcoal-burner, who gave him cheese and brown bread.\u000a\u000aThe King, his father, who was a good man, believed him; but his mother could not be persuaded it was true; and seeing that he went almost every day a-hunting, and that he always had some excuse ready for so doing, though he had lain out three or four nights together, she began to suspect that he was married, for he lived with the Princess above two whole years, and had by her two children, the eldest of which, who was a daughter, was named Morning, and the youngest, who was a son, they called Day, because he was a great deal handsomer and more beautiful than his sister.\u000a\u000aThe Queen spoke several times to her son, to inform herself after what manner he did pass his time, and that in this he ought in duty to satisfy her. But he never dared to trust her with his secret; he feared her, though he loved her, for she was of the race of the Ogres, and the King would never have married her had it not been for her vast riches; it was even whispered about the Court that she had Ogreish inclinations, and that, whenever she saw little children passing by, she had all the diﬀiculty in the world to avoid falling upon them. And so the Prince would never tell her one word.\u000a\u000aBut when the King was dead, which happened about two years afterward, and he saw himself lord and master, he openly declared his marriage; and he went in great ceremony to conduct his Queen to the palace. They made a magnificent entry into the capital city, she riding between her two children.\u000a\u000aSoon after the King went to make war with the Emperor Contalabutte, his neighbor. He left the government of the kingdom to the Queen his mother, and earnestly recommended to her care his wife and children. He was obliged to continue his expedition all the summer, and as soon as he departed the Queen-mother sent her daughter-in-law to a country house among the woods, that she might with the more ease gratify her horrible longing.\u000a\u000aSome few days afterward she went thither herself, and said to her clerk of the kitchen: “I have a mind to eat little Morning for my dinner to-morrow.” “Ah! madam,” cried the clerk of the kitchen. “I will have it so,” replied the Queen (and this she spoke in the tone of an Ogress who had a strong desire to eat fresh meat), “and will eat her with a sauce Robert.”\u000a\u000aThe poor man, knowing very well that he must not play tricks with Ogresses, took his great knife and went up into little Morning’s chamber. She was then four years old, and came up to him jumping and laughing, to take him about the neck, and ask him for some sugar-candy. Upon which he began to weep, the great knife fell out of his hand, and he went into the back yard, and killed a little lamb, and dressed it with such good sauce that his mistress assured him that she had never eaten anything so good in her life. He had at the same time taken'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('23d22f63-26f8-4d6d-a86d-3d36d9a2ce80','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',6,unistr('up little Morning, and carried her to his wife, to conceal her in the lodging he had at the bottom of the courtyard.\u000a\u000aAbout eight days afterward the wicked Queen said to the clerk of the kitchen, “I will sup on little Day.”\u000a\u000aHe answered not a word, being resolved to cheat her as he had done before. He went to find out little Day, and saw him with a little foil in his hand, with which he was fencing with a great monkey, the child being then only three years of age. He took him up in his arms and carried him to his wife, that she might conceal him in her chamber along with his sister, and in the room of little Day cooked up a young kid, very tender, which the Ogress found to be wonderfully good.\u000a\u000aThis was hitherto all mighty well; but one evening this wicked Queen said to her clerk of the kitchen:\u000a\u000a“I will eat the Queen with the same sauce I had with her children.”\u000a\u000aIt was now that the poor clerk of the kitchen despaired of being able to deceive her. The young Queen was turned of twenty, not reckoning the hundred years she had been asleep; and how to find in the yard a beast so firm was what puzzled him. He took then a resolution, that he might save his own life, to cut the Queen’s throat; and going up into her chamber, with intent to do it at once, he put himself into as great fury as he could possibly, and came into the young Queen’s room with his dagger in his hand. He would not, however, surprise her, but told her, with a great deal of respect, the orders he had received from the Queen-mother.\u000a\u000a“Do it; do it” (said she, stretching out her neck). “Execute your orders, and then I shall go and see my children, my poor children, whom I so much and so tenderly loved.”\u000a\u000aFor she thought them dead ever since they had been taken away without her knowledge.\u000a\u000a“No, no, madam” (cried the poor clerk of the kitchen, all in tears); “you shall not die, and yet you shall see your children again; but then you must go home with me to my lodgings, where I have concealed them, and I shall deceive the Queen once more, by giving her in your stead a young hind.”\u000a\u000aUpon this he forthwith conducted her to his chamber, where, leaving her to embrace her children, and cry along with them, he went and dressed a young hind, which the Queen had for her supper, and devoured it with the same appetite as if it had been the young Queen. Exceedingly was she delighted with her cruelty, and she had invented a story to tell the King, at his return, how the mad wolves had eaten up the Queen his wife and her two children.\u000a\u000aOne evening, as she was, according to her custom, rambling round about the courts and yards of the palace to see if she could smell any fresh meat, she heard, in a ground room, little Day crying, for his mamma was going to whip him, because he had been naughty; and she heard, at the same time, little Morning begging pardon for her brother.\u000a\u000aThe Ogress presently knew the voice of the Queen and her children, and being quite mad that she had been thus deceived, she commanded next morning, by break of day (with a most horrible voice, which made everybody tremble), that they should bring into the middle'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('384666b2-b128-4dfd-b396-da3b10b880a7','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',7,unistr('of the great court a large tub, which she caused to be filled with toads, vipers, snakes, and all sorts of serpents, in order to have thrown into it the Queen and her children, the clerk of the kitchen, his wife and maid; all whom she had given orders should be brought thither with their hands tied behind them.\u000a\u000aThey were brought out accordingly, and the executioners were just going to throw them into the tub, when the King (who was not so soon expected) entered the court on horseback (for he came post) and asked, with the utmost astonishment, what was the meaning of that horrible spectacle.\u000a\u000aNo one dared to tell him, when the Ogress, all enraged to see what had happened, threw herself head foremost into the tub, and was instantly devoured by the ugly creatures she had ordered to be thrown into it for others. The King could not but be very sorry, for she was his mother; but he soon comforted himself with his beautiful wife and his pretty children.\u000a\u000aPublic domain. From The Blue Fairy Book (ed. Andrew Lang, 1889); Project Gutenberg ebook #503.'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('9d434ccf-62dd-4b3b-bd6b-0fa901200c60','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',1,unistr('![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile1.png)\u000a\u000a# Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs\u000a\u000a14 15'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('04dfe90a-8d43-4ff9-89a7-c6db8bc3e301','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',2,unistr('## Part One\u000a\u000aOnce upon a time there was a little princess… She was called Snow White because she was white as snow. Her lips were red as blood. And her hair was black as night. She was a beautiful girl, sweet and gentle. She lived in a palace with her father and her stepmother: the King and the Queen. The Queen was a beautiful woman. But she was wicked and vain.\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile2.png)\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile3.png)\u000a\u000a*Every day, the Queen looked in her magic mirror and asked:*\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000aAnd every day, the mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“You, my Queen! You are! You are the most beautiful of all.”\u000a\u000aTime passed and little Snow White became a beautiful young lady. Everybody loved her. One morning, when the Queen asked her magic mirror:\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000a16 17'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('2a20d984-815d-42a8-a1d9-3adc9b4e4492','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',3,unistr('![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile4.png)\u000a\u000aThe mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“My Queen, you are beautiful, it’s true! But Snow White is more beautiful than you.”\u000a\u000aThe Queen was furious! She planned a horrible crime. She called a huntsman and commanded him: “Take Snow White deep into the forest… and kill her!” “But… Your Majesty! The beloved Princess!” he said. “Kill Snow White! And take out her heart,” the Queen commanded again.\u000a\u000a## Part Two\u000a\u000aThe huntsman took Snow White deep into the woods. “Forgive me, my Princess… I have orders from the Queen to kill you! But I can’t do that!” he said. “Oh, good man!” she cried, “I’ll run into the forest and I’ll never come back!” Snow White ran and ran through the dark forest. She was so scared! Then, she found a little house. The door was open. She went in.\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile5.png)\u000a\u000a18 19'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('ad0deec6-9294-4c34-906b-179ca1fbf3f1','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',4,unistr('![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile6.png)\u000a\u000aIn the morning, she woke up and saw the seven masters of the house looking at her. They were seven dwarfs. “Who are you?” they asked. “My name is Snow White,” she answered. And she told them the whole story about the wicked Queen. “Princess Snow White, you can stay,” the Dwarfs said. “Can you help us cook and clean?” “Yes! I can cook and I can wash. And I can make beds,” she said happily. “Please, remember: don’t open the door to strangers!” said the Dwarfs. “The Queen will find you.”\u000a\u000a*A little table was set for dinner: seven little chairs, seven little bowls, seven little spoons, seven little knives, seven little forks, and seven little mugs. Snow White was hungry and thirsty. She thought: “I’ll have a little bread from each bowl. And I’ll drink a little wine from each mug.” Then, feeling so tired, she lay down on one of the seven little beds. And she fell asleep.*\u000a\u000a20 21'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('97e17d62-aa73-4523-b138-f14156925ab7','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',5,unistr('## Part Three\u000a\u000aAt the Palace, the Queen asked her magic mirror:\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000aThe mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“My Queen, you are beautiful, it’s true! But Snow White is more beautiful than you. In the mountains, she dwells And she is still alive and well.”\u000a\u000aThe Queen was absolutely furious! “I will kill Snow White myself!” she thought. She made a deadly poison. Then, she put the poison in a beautiful red apple.\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile7.png)\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile8.png)\u000a\u000aThe Queen, dressed in old clothes, went to the little house in the mountains. She knocked on the door, calling: “APPLES! JUICY APPLES! APPLES FOR SALE!” Snow White answered: “I’m sorry, madam. I can’t open the door to strangers.” “Oh, I’m just an old woman! Open the window and take one apple,” said the Queen. Snow White opened the window, took the apple and had one bite. She fell dead to the floor! “Now, I’m the most beautiful of all. Ha, ha, ha!” the Queen said and she ran away.\u000a\u000a23'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_pages VALUES('b59403b7-2065-4ac9-847e-d68edb744e5e','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',6,unistr('![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile9.png)\u000a\u000aBack at the Palace, the Queen ran to her magic mirror:\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000aThe mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“You, my Queen! You are! You are the most beautiful of all.”\u000a\u000aThe wicked Queen was happy now because the magic mirror never lied.\u000a\u000a## Part Four\u000a\u000aThe Seven Dwarfs found Snow White dead on the floor. In her honor, they made her a glass coffin and placed it on top of a mountain. One day, a young Prince stopped to see her. And he fell in love with Snow White. With a kiss, she came back to a new life with her Prince. Snow White and the Prince had a big wedding. Everyone was invited to the celebration. Even the wicked Queen! And that night, when the Queen saw Snow White, she choked with envy and hate. And she fell dead to the floor. The Prince and the Princess lived happily ever after…\u000a\u000a### THE END\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile10.png)\u000a\u000a24 25'),NULL);
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('0546c32c-f5af-4006-8778-a41d108b9f37','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',0,unistr('# Cinderella, or the Little Glass Slipper\u000a\u000aOnce there was a gentleman who married, for his second wife, the proudest and most haughty woman that was ever seen. She had, by a former husband, two daughters of her own humor, who were, indeed, exactly like her in all things. He had likewise, by another wife, a young daughter, but of unparalleled goodness and sweetness of temper, which she took from her mother, who was the best creature in the world.\u000a\u000aNo sooner were the ceremonies of the wedding over but the mother-in-law began to show herself in her true colors. She could not bear the good qualities of this pretty girl, and the less because they made her own daughters appear the more odious. She employed her in the meanest work of the house: she scoured the dishes, tables, etc., and scrubbed madam’s chamber, and those of misses, her daughters; she lay up in a sorry garret, upon a wretched straw bed, while her sisters lay in fine rooms, with floors all inlaid, upon beds of the very newest fashion, and where they had looking-glasses so large that they might see themselves at their full length from head to foot.\u000a\u000aThe poor girl bore all patiently, and dared not tell her father, who would have rattled her off; for his wife governed him entirely. When she had done her work, she used to go into the chimney-corner, and sit down among cinders and ashes, which made her commonly be called Cinderwench; but the youngest, who was not so rude and uncivil as the eldest, called her Cinderella. However, Cinderella, notwithstanding her mean apparel, was a hundred times handsomer than her sisters, though they were always dressed very richly.'),1,0,411,'Cinderella, or the Little Glass Slipper','2026-05-28 21:13:10');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('0d02a4e2-4ca9-44a3-b93d-984dfab8bf2c','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',1,unistr('It happened that the King’s son gave a ball, and invited all persons of fashion to it. Our young misses were also invited, for they cut a very grand figure among the quality. They were mightily delighted at this invitation, and wonderfully busy in choosing out such gowns, petticoats, and head-clothes as might become them. This was a new trouble to Cinderella; for it was she who ironed her sisters’ linen, and plaited their ruffles; they talked all day long of nothing but how they should be dressed.\u000a\u000a“For my part,” said the eldest, “I will wear my red velvet suit with French trimming.”\u000a\u000a“And I,” said the youngest, “shall have my usual petticoat; but then, to make amends for that, I will put on my gold-flowered manteau, and my diamond stomacher, which is far from being the most ordinary one in the world.”\u000a\u000aThey sent for the best tire-woman they could get to make up their head-dresses and adjust their double pinners, and they had their red brushes and patches from Mademoiselle de la Poche.'),1,1647,250,'Cinderella, or the Little Glass Slipper','2026-05-28 21:13:10');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('f2da9fda-b7ad-485c-8afb-ac2054f90cc9','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',2,unistr('Cinderella was likewise called up to them to be consulted in all these matters, for she had excellent notions, and advised them always for the best, nay, and offered her services to dress their heads, which they were very willing she should do. As she was doing this, they said to her:\u000a\u000a“Cinderella, would you not be glad to go to the ball?” “Alas!” said she, “you only jeer me; it is not for such as I am to go thither.” “Thou art in the right of it,” replied they; “it would make the people laugh to see a Cinderwench at a ball.”\u000a\u000aAnyone but Cinderella would have dressed their heads awry, but she was very good, and dressed them perfectly well They were almost two days without eating, so much were they transported with joy. They broke above a dozen laces in trying to be laced up close, that they might have a fine slender shape, and they were continually at their looking-glass. At last the happy day came; they went to Court, and Cinderella followed them with her eyes as long as she could, and when she had lost sight of them, she fell a-crying.\u000a\u000aHer godmother, who saw her all in tears, asked her what was the matter. “I wish I could–I wish I could–“; she was not able to speak the rest, being interrupted by her tears and sobbing. This godmother of hers, who was a fairy, said to her, “Thou wishest thou couldst go to the ball; is it not so?” “Y–es,” cried Cinderella, with a great sigh.\u000a\u000a“Well,” said her godmother, “be but a good girl, and I will contrive that thou shalt go.” Then she took her into her chamber, and said to her, “Run into the garden, and bring me a pumpkin.”\u000a\u000aCinderella went immediately to gather the finest she could get, and brought it to her godmother, not being able to imagine how this pumpkin could make her go to the ball. Her godmother scooped out all the inside of it, having left nothing but the rind; which done, she struck it with her wand, and the pumpkin was instantly turned into a fine coach, gilded all over with gold.'),2,0,491,'','2026-05-28 21:13:10');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('6e6f507a-dbd2-4dc0-8b72-18d44e09fd54','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',3,unistr('Cinderella went immediately to gather the finest she could get, and brought it to her godmother, not being able to imagine how this pumpkin could make her go to the ball. Her godmother scooped out all the inside of it, having left nothing but the rind; which done, she struck it with her wand, and the pumpkin was instantly turned into a fine coach, gilded all over with gold.\u000a\u000aShe then went to look into her mouse-trap, where she found six mice, all alive, and ordered Cinderella to lift up a little the trapdoor, when, giving each mouse, as it went out, a little tap with her wand, the mouse was that moment turned into a fine horse, which altogether made a very fine set of six horses of a beautiful mouse-colored dapple-gray. Being at a loss for a coachman,\u000a\u000a“I will go and see,” says Cinderella, “if there is never a rat in the rat-trap–we may make a coachman of him.”\u000a\u000a“Thou art in the right,” replied her godmother; “go and look.”\u000a\u000aCinderella brought the trap to her, and in it there were three huge rats. The fairy made choice of one of the three which had the largest beard, and, having touched him with her'),2,1590,279,'','2026-05-28 21:13:10');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('a4afeff1-b5d0-46ad-b1da-c695dec785f6','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',4,unistr('wand, he was turned into a fat, jolly coachman, who had the smartest whiskers eyes ever beheld. After that, she said to her:\u000a\u000a“Go again into the garden, and you will find six lizards behind the watering-pot, bring them to me.”\u000a\u000aShe had no sooner done so but her godmother turned them into six footmen, who skipped up immediately behind the coach, with their liveries all bedaubed with gold and silver, and clung as close behind each other as if they had done nothing else their whole lives. The Fairy then said to Cinderella:\u000a\u000a“Well, you see here an equipage fit to go to the ball with; are you not pleased with it?” “Oh! yes,” cried she; “but must I go thither as I am, in these nasty rags?”\u000a\u000aHer godmother only just touched her with her wand, and, at the same instant, her clothes were turned into cloth of gold and silver, all beset with jewels. This done, she gave her a pair of glass slippers, the prettiest in the whole world. Being thus decked out, she got up into her coach; but her godmother, above all things, commanded her not to stay till after midnight, telling her, at the same time, that if she stayed one moment longer, the coach would be a pumpkin again, her horses mice, her coachman a rat, her footmen lizards, and her clothes become just as they were before.\u000a\u000aShe promised her godmother she would not fail of leaving the ball before midnight; and then away she drives, scarce able to contain herself for joy. The King’s son who was told that a great princess, whom nobody knew, was come, ran out to receive her; he gave her his hand\u000a\u000a- as she alighted out of the coach, and led her into the ball, among all the company. There was immediately a profound silence, they left off dancing, and the violins ceased to play, so attentive was everyone to contemplate the singular beauties of the unknown new-comer. Nothing was then heard but a confused noise of: “Ha! how handsome she is! Ha! how handsome she is!”'),3,0,481,'','2026-05-28 21:13:10');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('a3aca9d1-d649-4edc-92fe-e000a43a90f8','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',5,unistr('- as she alighted out of the coach, and led her into the ball, among all the company. There was immediately a profound silence, they left off dancing, and the violins ceased to play, so attentive was everyone to contemplate the singular beauties of the unknown new-comer. Nothing was then heard but a confused noise of: “Ha! how handsome she is! Ha! how handsome she is!”\u000a\u000aThe King himself, old as he was, could not help watching her, and telling the Queen softly that it was a long time since he had seen so beautiful and lovely a creature.\u000a\u000aAll the ladies were busied in considering her clothes and headdress, that they might have some made next day after the same pattern, provided they could meet with such fine material and as able hands to make them.\u000a\u000aThe King’s son conducted her to the most honorable seat, and afterward took her out to dance with him; she danced so very gracefully that they all more and more admired her. A fine collation was served up, whereof the young prince ate not a morsel, so intently was he busied in gazing on her.\u000a\u000aShe went and sat down by her sisters, showing them a thousand civilities, giving them part of the oranges and citrons which the Prince had presented her with, which very much surprised them, for they did not know her. While Cinderella was thus amusing her sisters, she heard the clock strike eleven and three-quarters, whereupon she immediately made a courtesy to the company and hasted away as fast as she could.'),3,1554,366,'','2026-05-28 21:13:10');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('2f4b00d5-a3f0-4df8-abc6-f2548573e48e','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',6,unistr('When she got home she ran to seek out her godmother, and, after having thanked her, she said she could not but heartily wish she might go next day to the ball, because the King’s son had desired her.\u000a\u000aAs she was eagerly telling her godmother whatever had passed at the ball, her two sisters knocked at the door, which Cinderella ran and opened.\u000a\u000a“How long you have stayed!” cried she, gaping, rubbing her eyes and stretching herself as if she had been just waked out of her sleep; she had not, however, any manner of inclination to sleep since they went from home.\u000a\u000a“If thou hadst been at the ball,” said one of her sisters, “thou wouldst not have been tired with it. There came thither the finest princess, the most beautiful ever was seen with mortal eyes; she showed us a thousand civilities, and gave us oranges and citrons.”\u000a\u000aCinderella seemed very indifferent in the matter; indeed, she asked them the name of that princess; but they told her they did not know it, and that the King’s son was very uneasy on her account and would give all the world to know who she was. At this Cinderella, smiling, replied:\u000a\u000a“She must, then, be very beautiful indeed; how happy you have been! Could not I see her? Ah! dear Miss Charlotte, do lend me your yellow suit of clothes which you wear every day.” “Ay, to be sure!” cried Miss Charlotte; “lend my clothes to such a dirty Cinderwench as thou art! I should be a fool.” Cinderella, indeed, expected well such answer, and was very glad of the refusal; for she would have been sadly put to it if her sister had lent her what she asked for jestingly.'),4,0,397,'','2026-05-28 21:13:10');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('f74b1c32-336e-4521-a781-a538bef44985','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',7,unistr('“She must, then, be very beautiful indeed; how happy you have been! Could not I see her? Ah! dear Miss Charlotte, do lend me your yellow suit of clothes which you wear every day.” “Ay, to be sure!” cried Miss Charlotte; “lend my clothes to such a dirty Cinderwench as thou art! I should be a fool.” Cinderella, indeed, expected well such answer, and was very glad of the refusal; for she would have been sadly put to it if her sister had lent her what she asked for jestingly.\u000a\u000aThe next day the two sisters were at the ball, and so was Cinderella, but dressed more magnificently than before. The King’s son was always by her, and never ceased his compliments and kind speeches to her; to whom all this was so far from being tiresome that she quite forgot what her godmother had recommended to her; so that she, at last, counted the clock striking twelve when she took it to be no more than eleven; she then rose up and fled, as nimble as a deer. The Prince followed, but could not overtake her. She left behind one of her glass slippers, which the Prince took up most carefully. She got home but quite out of breath, and in her nasty old clothes, having nothing left her of all her finery but one of the little slippers, fellow to that she dropped. The guards at the palace gate were asked:\u000a\u000aIf they had not seen a princess go out. Who said: They had seen nobody go out but a young girl, very meanly dressed, and who had more the air of a poor country wench than a gentlewoman. When the two sisters returned from the ball Cinderella asked them: If they had been well diverted, and if the fine lady had been there.\u000a\u000aThey told her: Yes, but that she hurried away immediately when it struck twelve, and with so much haste that she dropped one of her little glass slippers, the prettiest in the world, which the King’s son had taken up; that he had done nothing but look at her all the time'),4,1115,471,'','2026-05-28 21:13:10');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('c835e1aa-8233-45bf-8102-d7ba946f6653','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',8,unistr('- at the ball, and that most certainly he was very much in love with the beautiful person who owned the glass slipper.\u000a\u000aWhat they said was very true; for a few days after the King’s son caused it to be proclaimed, by sound of trumpet, that he would marry her whose foot the slipper would just fit. They whom he employed began to try it upon the princesses, then the duchesses and all the Court, but in vain; it was brought to the two sisters, who did all they possibly could to thrust their foot into the slipper, but they could not effect it. Cinderella, who saw all this, and knew her slipper, said to them, laughing:\u000a\u000a“Let me see if it will not fit me.” Her sisters burst out a-laughing, and began to banter her. The gentleman who was sent to try the slipper looked earnestly at Cinderella, and, finding her very handsome, said: It was but just that she should try, and that he had orders to let everyone make trial.\u000a\u000aHe obliged Cinderella to sit down, and, putting the slipper to her foot, he found it went on very easily, and fitted her as if it had been made of wax. The astonishment her two sisters were in was excessively great, but still abundantly greater when Cinderella pulled out of her pocket the other slipper, and put it on her foot. Thereupon, in came her godmother, who, having touched with her wand Cinderella’s clothes, made them richer and more magnificent than any of those she had before.\u000a\u000aAnd now her two sisters found her to be that fine, beautiful lady whom they had seen at the ball. They threw themselves at her feet to beg pardon for all the ill-treatment they had made her undergo. Cinderella took them up, and, as she embraced them, cried:\u000a\u000aThat she forgave them with all her heart, and desired them always to love her.\u000a\u000aShe was conducted to the young prince, dressed as she was; he thought her more charming than ever, and, a few days after, married her. Cinderella, who was no less good than beautiful, gave her two sisters lodgings in the palace, and that very same day matched them with two great lords of the Court.(1)\u000a\u000a(1) Charles Perrault.'),5,0,519,'','2026-05-28 21:13:10');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('f9e279be-4338-4ce5-a6c8-e86f926328c2','9d474ffd-9678-4a8a-a606-353eb24080b3',9,unistr('That she forgave them with all her heart, and desired them always to love her.\u000a\u000aShe was conducted to the young prince, dressed as she was; he thought her more charming than ever, and, a few days after, married her. Cinderella, who was no less good than beautiful, gave her two sisters lodgings in the palace, and that very same day matched them with two great lords of the Court.(1)\u000a\u000a(1) Charles Perrault.\u000a\u000aPublic domain. From The Blue Fairy Book (ed. Andrew Lang, 1889); Project Gutenberg ebook #503.'),5,1672,125,'','2026-05-28 21:13:10');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('dc1fcb82-de9f-44d4-8d56-d1f8f96d48c8','ffdca276-4f06-4195-a43e-5bb1fd3cb3c7',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Cinderella.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Cinderella\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aCinderella is a central character in a well-known fairy tale, symbolizing resilience and virtue amidst adversity. Her story highlights themes of kindness, hope, and transformation.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Represents resilience in overcoming hardship.\u000a- Embodies virtues such as kindness and humility.\u000a- Often depicted as mistreated by her stepfamily.\u000a- Experiences a significant transformation through magical intervention.\u000a- Ultimately rewarded for her goodness and perseverance.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aCinderella is a prominent figure in folklore and fairy tales, often serving as an archetype for the "rags to riches" narrative. Her story has been adapted into various cultural forms, including literature, theater, and film, influencing the portrayal of female protagonists in literature and popular media.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Cinderella.pdf'),1,0,227,'Cinderella > Sources','2026-05-28 21:13:18');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('039f4a0d-3ad2-4ad8-8fd2-030b85c38d64','e834a6f0-141e-459f-9b25-25a4b99b1b59',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Cinderella.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Fairy Godmother\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Fairy Godmother is a magical figure in folklore who provides assistance to the protagonist, often in times of need. In the context of the story of Cinderella, she symbolizes hope and the transformative power of kindness.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Magical abilities to grant wishes or provide assistance.\u000a- Acts as a mentor or guide to the protagonist.\u000a- Represents themes of hope, kindness, and transformation.\u000a- Often depicted as a benevolent and nurturing figure.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe concept of the Fairy Godmother is prevalent in various fairy tales and folklore, serving as a narrative device to highlight the importance of kindness and the potential for change in one''s circumstances. In the broader domain of fairy tales, she embodies the archetype of the helper, reinforcing the idea that good deeds and compassion can lead to positive outcomes.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Cinderella.pdf'),1,0,241,'Fairy Godmother > Sources','2026-05-28 21:13:21');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('ed101cca-9444-4b52-96c4-2c2dd27dd0ed','c8f483c8-6188-4716-bfe1-f553b98c7946',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [instrument]\u000asources: [Cinderella.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Glass Slipper\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe glass slipper is a magical footwear item featured in the fairy tale "Cinderella," serving as a crucial plot device that enables Cinderella''s identification and subsequent marriage to the prince.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Made of glass, symbolizing purity and beauty.\u000a- Fits only Cinderella, highlighting her uniqueness.\u000a- Represents transformation and the fulfillment of dreams.\u000a- Acts as a catalyst for the resolution of the story.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe glass slipper is emblematic of the broader themes of fairy tales, including the triumph of good over evil, the importance of identity, and the transformative power of love. It underscores the narrative''s focus on self-discovery and the idea that true worth is recognized through genuine connection.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Cinderella.pdf'),1,0,216,'Glass Slipper > Sources','2026-05-28 21:13:23');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('a2eb1dab-9048-4f88-b5e9-5ffb5f83fecc','88ff625f-5833-4549-9800-11493b389186',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [Cinderella.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Transformation\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aTransformation refers to a significant change in form, appearance, or character. In the context of storytelling, it often symbolizes personal growth or a shift in circumstances.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Represents a shift from a lower to a higher status, as seen in Cinderella''s journey from oppression to royalty.\u000a- Often involves external changes (e.g., physical appearance, social status) that reflect internal growth or change.\u000a- Can be triggered by specific events or interventions, such as magical assistance or personal resilience.\u000a- Highlights themes of hope, redemption, and the possibility of change.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe theme of transformation is a common motif in literature, illustrating the potential for change in characters'' lives. In "Cinderella," it serves to emphasize the contrast between despair and hope, showcasing the journey from hardship to fulfillment. This theme resonates with audiences by reflecting universal desires for improvement and the belief in the possibility of a better future.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Cinderella.pdf'),1,0,281,'Transformation > Sources','2026-05-28 21:13:31');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('561203ec-a0c2-4e08-9f7a-c30710d3d142','a4bab150-df3c-4f26-926d-a7ba78360039',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [Cinderella.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Forgiveness\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aForgiveness is the process of letting go of resentment or anger towards someone who has caused harm or distress. In the context of storytelling, it often reflects a character''s moral strength and capacity for compassion.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Involves the release of negative emotions such as anger and resentment.\u000a- Often requires a sense of empathy or understanding towards the offender.\u000a- Can lead to personal growth and healing for the forgiver.\u000a- May be depicted as a noble or virtuous act in literature and narratives.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aForgiveness is a prevalent theme in literature and moral teachings, often serving as a catalyst for character development and resolution of conflict. In "Cinderella," the protagonist''s ability to forgive her stepsisters for their mistreatment highlights her noble character and reinforces the idea that forgiveness can lead to reconciliation and personal dignity.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Cinderella.pdf'),1,0,252,'Forgiveness > Sources','2026-05-28 21:13:35');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('effd1f3d-4c38-4e43-91ba-3ab099ff41cd','db7750bd-01f2-44fe-921e-407c2296d6f1',0,unistr('# Cinderella\u000a\u000a**Source:** Cinderella.pdf | **Type:** pdf | **Pages:** 5 | **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a\u000a## Summary\u000a\u000aThe story of Cinderella follows a young girl mistreated by her stepmother and stepsisters who, with the help of her fairy godmother, attends a royal ball and captures the heart of the prince. After losing a glass slipper during her hurried departure, the prince searches for her, ultimately leading to her recognition and a happy ending. The tale emphasizes themes of kindness, transformation, and forgiveness.\u000a\u000a## Related Concepts\u000a\u000a- [Cinderella](../concepts/cinderella.md)\u000a- [Fairy Godmother](../concepts/fairy-godmother.md)\u000a- [Glass Slipper](../concepts/glass-slipper.md)\u000a- [Transformation](../concepts/transformation.md)\u000a- [Forgiveness](../concepts/forgiveness.md)\u000a\u000a## Source Information\u000a\u000a- **File:** Cinderella.pdf\u000a- **Type:** pdf\u000a- **Pages:** 5\u000a- **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a- **Parser:** opendataloader\u000a\u000a[^1]: Cinderella.pdf'),1,0,235,'Cinderella > Source Information','2026-05-28 21:13:35');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('c871191d-436a-4386-94e5-15ffef93520e','fd3ae084-fbd2-4de8-a63a-4c671574d943',0,unistr('# Little Red Riding Hood\u000a\u000aOnce upon a time there lived in a certain village a little country girl, the prettiest creature was ever seen. Her mother was excessively fond of her; and her grandmother doted on her still more. This good woman had made for her a little red riding-hood; which became the girl so extremely well that everybody called her Little Red Riding-Hood.\u000a\u000aOne day her mother, having made some custards, said to her: “Go, my dear, and see how thy grandmamma does, for I hear she has been very ill; carry her a custard, and this little pot of butter.” Little Red Riding-Hood set out immediately to go to her grandmother, who lived in another village.\u000a\u000aAs she was going through the wood, she met with Gaffer Wolf, who had a very great mind to eat her up, but he dared not, because of some faggot-makers hard by in the forest. He asked her whither she was going. The poor child, who did not know that it was dangerous to stay and hear a wolf talk, said to him:\u000a\u000a“I am going to see my grandmamma and carry her a custard and a little pot of butter from my mamma.”\u000a\u000a“Does she live far off?” said the Wolf. “Oh! ay,” answered Little Red Riding-Hood; “it is beyond that mill you see there, at the first house in the village.” “Well,” said the Wolf, “and I’ll go and see her too. I’ll go this way and you go that, and we shall see who will be there soonest.”\u000a\u000aThe Wolf began to run as fast as he could, taking the nearest way, and the little girl went by that farthest about, diverting herself in gathering nuts, running after butterflies, and making nosegays of such little flowers as she met with. The Wolf was not long before he got to the old woman’s house. He knocked at the door–tap, tap.\u000a\u000a“Who’s there?” “Your grandchild, Little Red Riding-Hood,” replied the Wolf, counterfeiting her voice; “who has brought you a custard and a little pot of butter sent you by mamma.” The good grandmother, who was in bed, because she was somewhat ill, cried out:'),1,0,490,'Little Red Riding Hood','2026-05-28 21:13:40');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('031c6f40-9147-44ae-9d25-54eec3adef93','fd3ae084-fbd2-4de8-a63a-4c671574d943',1,unistr('“Pull the bobbin, and the latch will go up.”\u000a\u000aThe Wolf pulled the bobbin, and the door opened, and then presently he fell upon the good woman and ate her up in a moment, for it was above three days that he had not touched a bit. He then shut the door and went into the grandmother’s bed, expecting Little Red Riding-Hood, who came some time afterward and knocked at the door–tap, tap.\u000a\u000a“Who’s there?” Little Red Riding-Hood, hearing the big voice of the Wolf, was at first afraid; but believing her grandmother had got a cold and was hoarse, answered: “‘Tis your grandchild, Little Red Riding-Hood, who has brought you a custard and a little pot of butter mamma sends you.” The Wolf cried out to her, softening his voice as much as he could: “Pull the bobbin, and the latch will go up.” Little Red Riding-Hood pulled the bobbin, and the door opened. The Wolf, seeing her come in, said to her, hiding himself under the bed-clothes: “Put the custard and the little pot of butter upon the stool, and come and lie down with me.” Little Red Riding-Hood undressed herself and went into bed, where, being greatly amazed to see how her grandmother looked in her night-clothes, she said to her: “Grandmamma, what great arms you have got!” “That is the better to hug thee, my dear.” “Grandmamma, what great legs you have got!” “That is to run the better, my child.” “Grandmamma, what great ears you have got!” “That is to hear the better, my child.” “Grandmamma, what great eyes you have got!” “It is to see the better, my child.” “Grandmamma, what great teeth you have got!” “That is to eat thee up.” And, saying these words, this wicked wolf fell upon Little Red Riding-Hood, and ate her all up.\u000a\u000aPublic domain. From The Blue Fairy Book (ed. Andrew Lang, 1889); Project Gutenberg ebook #503.'),2,0,445,'','2026-05-28 21:13:40');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('7a93abf1-2805-4e80-a2ea-2c86cf7aaaa5','bb1e9772-ad9b-428c-9ed7-793b234c7ca7',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Little Red Riding Hood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Little Red Riding Hood\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aLittle Red Riding Hood is a classic fairy tale character recognized for her iconic red cloak and the moral lessons that arise from her interactions with a wolf.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Wears a distinctive red cloak.\u000a- Represents innocence and naivety.\u000a- Encounters a wolf that symbolizes danger and deception.\u000a- The story often conveys moral lessons about caution and the consequences of disobedience.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aLittle Red Riding Hood is part of the broader tradition of fairy tales that often feature anthropomorphic characters and moral lessons. The tale has been adapted and retold in various cultures, highlighting themes of vulnerability and the importance of heeding warnings.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Little Red Riding Hood.pdf'),1,0,207,'Little Red Riding Hood > Sources','2026-05-28 21:13:47');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('d65fd815-7096-4cd3-8cb1-87b704e2f12f','1a9107f8-3cfb-418d-9c56-0cb73137ea05',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Little Red Riding Hood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# The Wolf\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Wolf is a character in the fairy tale "Little Red Riding Hood," symbolizing cunning and predatory behavior. As the antagonist, the Wolf deceives and ultimately consumes both Little Red Riding Hood and her grandmother.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Represents cunning and deceitfulness.\u000a- Acts as the primary antagonist in the story.\u000a- Engages in manipulation to achieve its goals.\u000a- Embodies predatory instincts, preying on the innocent.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe Wolf serves as a critical figure in the narrative of "Little Red Riding Hood," illustrating themes of danger and the consequences of naivety. This character reflects broader archetypes of villainy in folklore, where predators often symbolize the threats that exist in the world, particularly those that target the vulnerable.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Little Red Riding Hood.pdf'),1,0,227,'The Wolf > Sources','2026-05-28 21:13:50');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('d2388184-a5aa-4c24-a007-9669c92bf93d','acfc1927-f028-476f-ac4a-89bf82df1eb3',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [Little Red Riding Hood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Cautionary Tales\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aCautionary tales are narratives that serve to warn readers about specific dangers or moral lessons, often highlighting the consequences of certain behaviors or decisions. They typically aim to instill a sense of caution and awareness in the audience.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- **Moral Lesson**: Each tale conveys a clear moral or lesson, often related to safety or ethical behavior.\u000a- **Warning Against Naivety**: Many cautionary tales emphasize the dangers of being naive or trusting, particularly in unfamiliar situations.\u000a- **Consequences**: The stories often depict negative outcomes that result from ignoring the warnings, reinforcing the importance of caution.\u000a- **Engaging Narrative**: They are typically engaging and memorable, using vivid characters and scenarios to illustrate their points.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aCautionary tales are a significant aspect of folklore and literature, often used in educational settings to teach children about safety and morality. They relate to broader themes of social norms and values, serving as tools for cultural transmission and behavioral guidance.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Little Red Riding Hood.pdf'),1,0,306,'Cautionary Tales > Sources','2026-05-28 21:13:53');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('314a35fd-8428-4342-8595-5a8022dc2c51','eb4e247c-475a-4530-813b-85fc70cb4a11',0,unistr('# Little Red Riding Hood\u000a\u000a**Source:** Little Red Riding Hood.pdf | **Type:** pdf | **Pages:** 2 | **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a\u000a## Summary\u000a\u000aThe story of Little Red Riding Hood follows a young girl who is sent by her mother to visit her sick grandmother, carrying custards and butter. On her way, she encounters a wolf who tricks her and ultimately devours both her and her grandmother. The tale serves as a cautionary narrative about the dangers of naivety and the importance of being wary of strangers.\u000a\u000a## Related Concepts\u000a\u000a- [Little Red Riding Hood](../concepts/little-red-riding-hood.md)\u000a- [The Wolf](../concepts/the-wolf.md)\u000a- [Cautionary Tales](../concepts/cautionary-tales.md)\u000a- [Fairy Tales](../concepts/fairy-tales.md)\u000a\u000a## Source Information\u000a\u000a- **File:** Little Red Riding Hood.pdf\u000a- **Type:** pdf\u000a- **Pages:** 2\u000a- **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a- **Parser:** opendataloader\u000a\u000a[^1]: Little Red Riding Hood.pdf'),1,0,226,'Little Red Riding Hood > Source Information','2026-05-28 21:13:56');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('727df824-7f77-42b7-8db3-45cb9554fe6a','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',0,unistr('# The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood\u000a\u000aThere were formerly a king and a queen, who were so sorry that they had no children; so sorry that it cannot be expressed. They went to all the waters in the world; vows, pilgrimages, all ways were tried, and all to no purpose.\u000a\u000aAt last, however, the Queen had a daughter. There was a very fine christening; and the Princess had for her god-mothers all the fairies they could find in the whole kingdom (they found seven), that every one of them might give her a gift, as was the custom of fairies in those days. By this means the Princess had all the perfections imaginable.\u000a\u000aAfter the ceremonies of the christening were over, all the company returned to the King’s palace, where was prepared a great feast for the fairies. There was placed before every one of them a magnificent cover with a case of massive gold, wherein were a spoon, knife, and fork, all of pure gold set with diamonds and rubies. But as they were all sitting down at table they saw come into the hall a very old fairy, whom they had not invited, because it was above fifty years since she had been out of a certain tower, and she was believed to be either dead or enchanted.\u000a\u000aThe King ordered her a cover, but could not furnish her with a case of gold as the others, because they had only seven made for the seven fairies. The old Fairy fancied she was slighted, and muttered some threats between her teeth. One of the young fairies who sat by her overheard how she grumbled; and, judging that she might give the little Princess some unlucky gift, went, as soon as they rose from table, and hid herself behind the hangings, that she might speak last, and repair, as much as she could, the evil which the old Fairy might intend.'),1,0,433,'The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('3b73434a-e83a-49e1-8f34-5d85863ab035','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',1,unistr('In the meanwhile all the fairies began to give their gifts to the Princess. The youngest gave her for gift that she should be the most beautiful person in the world; the next, that she should have the wit of an angel; the third, that she should have a wonderful grace in everything she did; the fourth, that she should dance perfectly well; the fifth, that she should sing like a nightingale; and the sixth, that she should play all kinds of music to the utmost perfection.\u000a\u000aThe old Fairy’s turn coming next, with a head shaking more with spite than age, she said that the Princess should have her hand pierced with a spindle and die of the wound. This terrible gift made the whole company tremble, and everybody fell a-crying.\u000a\u000aAt this very instant the young Fairy came out from behind the hangings, and spake these words aloud:'),1,1735,207,'The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('6993473d-ed18-4b0f-b297-02e1b3cb14bf','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',2,unistr('“Assure yourselves, O King and Queen, that your daughter shall not die of this disaster. It is true, I have no power to undo entirely what my elder has done. The Princess shall indeed pierce her hand with a spindle; but, instead of dying, she shall only fall into a profound sleep, which shall last a hundred years, at the expiration of which a king’s son shall come and awake her.”\u000a\u000aThe King, to avoid the misfortune foretold by the old Fairy, caused immediately proclamation to be made, whereby everybody was forbidden, on pain of death, to spin with a distaff and spindle, or to have so much as any spindle in their houses. About fifteen or sixteen years after, the King and Queen being gone to one of their houses of pleasure, the young Princess happened one day to divert herself in running up and down the palace; when going up from one apartment to another, she came into a little room on the top of the tower, where a good old woman, alone, was spinning with her spindle. This good woman had never heard of the King’s proclamation against spindles.\u000a\u000a“What are you doing there, goody?” said the Princess. “I am spinning, my pretty child,” said the old woman, who did not know who she was. “Ha!” said the Princess, “this is very pretty; how do you do it? Give it to me, that I may see if I can do so.”\u000a\u000aShe had no sooner taken it into her hand than, whether being very hasty at it, somewhat unhandy, or that the decree of the Fairy had so ordained it, it ran into her hand, and she fell down in a swoon.\u000a\u000aThe good old woman, not knowing very well what to do in this affair, cried out for help. People came in from every quarter in great numbers; they threw water upon the Princess’s face, unlaced her, struck her on the palms of her hands, and rubbed her temples with Hungarywater; but nothing would bring her to herself.'),2,0,456,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('05b37213-1d37-40cb-909b-db207ff63a74','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',3,unistr('The good old woman, not knowing very well what to do in this affair, cried out for help. People came in from every quarter in great numbers; they threw water upon the Princess’s face, unlaced her, struck her on the palms of her hands, and rubbed her temples with Hungarywater; but nothing would bring her to herself.\u000a\u000aAnd now the King, who came up at the noise, bethought himself of the prediction of the fairies, and, judging very well that this must necessarily come to pass, since the fairies had said it, caused the Princess to be carried into the finest apartment in his palace, and to be laid upon a bed all embroidered with gold and silver.\u000a\u000aOne would have taken her for a little angel, she was so very beautiful; for her swooning away had not diminished one bit of her complexion; her cheeks were carnation, and her lips were coral; indeed, her eyes were shut, but she was heard to breathe softly, which satisfied those about her that she was not dead. The King commanded that they should not disturb her, but let her sleep quietly till her hour of awaking was come.\u000a\u000aThe good Fairy who had saved her life by condemning her to sleep a hundred years was in the kingdom of Matakin, twelve thousand leagues off, when this accident befell the Princess; but she was instantly informed of it by a little dwarf, who had boots of seven leagues, that is, boots with which he could tread over seven leagues of ground in one stride. The Fairy came away immediately, and she arrived, about an hour after, in a fiery chariot drawn by dragons.\u000a\u000aThe King handed her out of the chariot, and she approved everything he had done, but as she had very great foresight, she thought when the Princess should awake she might not know'),2,1511,429,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('b3f0624f-bb47-4f57-88ad-47dbdab4699c','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',4,unistr('what to do with herself, being all alone in this old palace; and this was what she did: she touched with her wand everything in the palace (except the King and Queen)–governesses, maids of honor, ladies of the bedchamber, gentlemen, oﬀicers, stewards, cooks, undercooks, scullions, guards, with their beefeaters, pages, footmen; she likewise touched all the horses which were in the stables, pads as well as others, the great dogs in the outward court and pretty little Mopsey too, the Princess’s little spaniel, which lay by her on the bed.\u000a\u000aImmediately upon her touching them they all fell asleep, that they might not awake before their mistress and that they might be ready to wait upon her when she wanted them. The very spits at the fire, as full as they could hold of partridges and pheasants, did fall asleep also. All this was done in a moment. Fairies are not long in doing their business.\u000a\u000aAnd now the King and the Queen, having kissed their dear child without waking her, went out of the palace and put forth a proclamation that nobody should dare to come near it.\u000a\u000aThis, however, was not necessary, for in a quarter of an hour’s time there grew up all round about the park such a vast number of trees, great and small, bushes and brambles, twining one within another, that neither man nor beast could pass through; so that nothing could be seen but the very top of the towers of the palace; and that, too, not unless it was a good way off. Nobody; doubted but the Fairy gave herein a very extraordinary sample of her art, that the Princess, while she continued sleeping, might have nothing to fear from any curious people.\u000a\u000aWhen a hundred years were gone and passed the son of the King then reigning, and who was of another family from that of the sleeping Princess, being gone a-hunting on that side of the country, asked:'),3,0,458,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('a51b96c6-dc5e-4e81-bcb3-1d912bb1cf1e','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',5,unistr('When a hundred years were gone and passed the son of the King then reigning, and who was of another family from that of the sleeping Princess, being gone a-hunting on that side of the country, asked:\u000a\u000aWhat those towers were which he saw in the middle of a great thick wood? Everyone answered according as they had heard. Some said: That it was a ruinous old castle, haunted by spirits. Others, That all the sorcerers and witches of the country kept there their sabbath or night’s meeting.\u000a\u000aThe common opinion was: That an ogre lived there, and that he carried thither all the little children he could catch, that he might eat them up at his leisure, without anybody being able to follow him, as having himself only the power to pass through the wood.\u000a\u000aThe Prince was at a stand, not knowing what to believe, when a very good countryman spake to him thus:\u000a\u000a“May it please your royal highness, it is now about fifty years since I heard from my father, who heard my grandfather say, that there was then in this castle a princess, the most beautiful was ever seen; that she must sleep there a hundred years, and should be waked by a king’s son, for whom she was reserved.”\u000a\u000aThe young Prince was all on fire at these words, believing, without weighing the matter, that he could put an end to this rare adventure; and, pushed on by love and honor, resolved that moment to look into it.'),3,1636,344,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('197968c0-0de5-4584-ac52-37424d820ab9','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',6,unistr('Scarce had he advanced toward the wood when all the great trees, the bushes, and brambles gave way of themselves to let him pass through; he walked up to the castle which he saw at the end of a large avenue which he went into; and what a little surprised him was that he saw none of his people could follow him, because the trees closed again as soon as he had passed through them. However, he did not cease from continuing his way; a young and amorous prince is always valiant.\u000a\u000aHe came into a spacious outward court, where everything he saw might have frozen the most fearless person with horror. There reigned all over a most frightful silence; the image of death everywhere showed itself, and there was nothing to be seen but stretched-out bodies of men and animals, all seeming to be dead. He, however, very well knew, by the ruby faces and pimpled noses of the beefeaters, that they were only asleep; and their goblets, wherein still remained some drops of wine, showed plainly that they fell asleep in their cups.\u000a\u000aHe then crossed a court paved with marble, went up the stairs and came into the guard chamber, where guards were standing in their ranks, with their muskets upon their shoulders, and snoring as loud as they could. After that he went through several rooms full of gentlemen and ladies, all asleep, some standing, others sitting. At last he came into a chamber all gilded with gold, where he saw upon a bed, the curtains of which were all open, the finest sight was ever beheld–a princess, who appeared to be about fifteen or sixteen years of age, and whose bright and, in a manner, resplendent beauty, had somewhat in it divine. He approached with trembling and admiration, and fell down before her upon his knees.\u000a\u000aAnd now, as the enchantment was at an end, the Princess awaked, and looking on him with eyes more tender than the first view might seem to admit of:\u000a\u000a“Is it you, my Prince?” said she to him. “You have waited a long while.”'),4,0,489,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('8fdc05e4-a6d0-4cc0-bb31-8f74529ed9db','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',7,unistr('And now, as the enchantment was at an end, the Princess awaked, and looking on him with eyes more tender than the first view might seem to admit of:\u000a\u000a“Is it you, my Prince?” said she to him. “You have waited a long while.”\u000a\u000aThe Prince, charmed with these words, and much more with the manner in which they were spoken, knew not how to show his joy and gratitude; he assured her that he loved her better than he did himself; their discourse was not well connected, they did weep more than talk– little eloquence, a great deal of love. He was more at a loss than she, and we need not wonder at it; she had time to think on what to say to him; for it is very probable (though history mentions nothing of it) that the good Fairy, during so long a sleep, had given her very agreeable dreams. In short, they talked four hours together, and yet they said not half what they had to say.\u000a\u000aIn the meanwhile all the palace awaked; everyone thought upon their particular business, and as all of them were not in love they were ready to die for hunger. The chief lady of honor, being as sharp set as other folks, grew very impatient, and told the Princess aloud that supper was served up. The Prince helped the Princess to rise; she was entirely dressed, and very magnificently, but his royal highness took care not to tell her that she was dressed like his great-grandmother, and had a point band peeping over a high collar; she looked not a bit less charming and beautiful for all that.\u000a\u000aThey went into the great hall of looking-glasses, where they supped, and were served by the Princess’s oﬀicers, the violins and hautboys played old tunes, but very excellent, though it was now above a hundred years since they had played; and after supper, without losing any'),4,1737,437,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('ac5ce656-c108-4650-82d1-a328c8105fc5','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',8,unistr('time, the lord almoner married them in the chapel of the castle, and the chief lady of honor drew the curtains. They had but very little sleep–the Princess had no occasion; and the Prince left her next morning to return to the city, where his father must needs have been in pain for him. The Prince told him:\u000a\u000aThat he lost his way in the forest as he was hunting, and that he had lain in the cottage of a charcoal-burner, who gave him cheese and brown bread.\u000a\u000aThe King, his father, who was a good man, believed him; but his mother could not be persuaded it was true; and seeing that he went almost every day a-hunting, and that he always had some excuse ready for so doing, though he had lain out three or four nights together, she began to suspect that he was married, for he lived with the Princess above two whole years, and had by her two children, the eldest of which, who was a daughter, was named Morning, and the youngest, who was a son, they called Day, because he was a great deal handsomer and more beautiful than his sister.\u000a\u000aThe Queen spoke several times to her son, to inform herself after what manner he did pass his time, and that in this he ought in duty to satisfy her. But he never dared to trust her with his secret; he feared her, though he loved her, for she was of the race of the Ogres, and the King would never have married her had it not been for her vast riches; it was even whispered about the Court that she had Ogreish inclinations, and that, whenever she saw little children passing by, she had all the diﬀiculty in the world to avoid falling upon them. And so the Prince would never tell her one word.\u000a\u000aBut when the King was dead, which happened about two years afterward, and he saw himself lord and master, he openly declared his marriage; and he went in great ceremony to conduct his Queen to the palace. They made a magnificent entry into the capital city, she riding between her two children.'),5,0,482,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('a194b5ec-003d-4008-8caa-7d4c4cafedef','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',9,unistr('But when the King was dead, which happened about two years afterward, and he saw himself lord and master, he openly declared his marriage; and he went in great ceremony to conduct his Queen to the palace. They made a magnificent entry into the capital city, she riding between her two children.\u000a\u000aSoon after the King went to make war with the Emperor Contalabutte, his neighbor. He left the government of the kingdom to the Queen his mother, and earnestly recommended to her care his wife and children. He was obliged to continue his expedition all the summer, and as soon as he departed the Queen-mother sent her daughter-in-law to a country house among the woods, that she might with the more ease gratify her horrible longing.\u000a\u000aSome few days afterward she went thither herself, and said to her clerk of the kitchen: “I have a mind to eat little Morning for my dinner to-morrow.” “Ah! madam,” cried the clerk of the kitchen. “I will have it so,” replied the Queen (and this she spoke in the tone of an Ogress who had a strong desire to eat fresh meat), “and will eat her with a sauce Robert.”\u000a\u000aThe poor man, knowing very well that he must not play tricks with Ogresses, took his great knife and went up into little Morning’s chamber. She was then four years old, and came up to him jumping and laughing, to take him about the neck, and ask him for some sugar-candy. Upon which he began to weep, the great knife fell out of his hand, and he went into the back yard, and killed a little lamb, and dressed it with such good sauce that his mistress assured him that she had never eaten anything so good in her life. He had at the same time taken'),5,1635,410,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('97acd257-e808-4182-b09a-3cd9e8bf7191','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',10,unistr('up little Morning, and carried her to his wife, to conceal her in the lodging he had at the bottom of the courtyard.\u000a\u000aAbout eight days afterward the wicked Queen said to the clerk of the kitchen, “I will sup on little Day.”\u000a\u000aHe answered not a word, being resolved to cheat her as he had done before. He went to find out little Day, and saw him with a little foil in his hand, with which he was fencing with a great monkey, the child being then only three years of age. He took him up in his arms and carried him to his wife, that she might conceal him in her chamber along with his sister, and in the room of little Day cooked up a young kid, very tender, which the Ogress found to be wonderfully good.\u000a\u000aThis was hitherto all mighty well; but one evening this wicked Queen said to her clerk of the kitchen:\u000a\u000a“I will eat the Queen with the same sauce I had with her children.”\u000a\u000aIt was now that the poor clerk of the kitchen despaired of being able to deceive her. The young Queen was turned of twenty, not reckoning the hundred years she had been asleep; and how to find in the yard a beast so firm was what puzzled him. He took then a resolution, that he might save his own life, to cut the Queen’s throat; and going up into her chamber, with intent to do it at once, he put himself into as great fury as he could possibly, and came into the young Queen’s room with his dagger in his hand. He would not, however, surprise her, but told her, with a great deal of respect, the orders he had received from the Queen-mother.\u000a\u000a“Do it; do it” (said she, stretching out her neck). “Execute your orders, and then I shall go and see my children, my poor children, whom I so much and so tenderly loved.”\u000a\u000aFor she thought them dead ever since they had been taken away without her knowledge.\u000a\u000a“No, no, madam” (cried the poor clerk of the kitchen, all in tears); “you shall not die, and yet you shall see your children again; but then you must go home with me to my lodgings, where I have concealed them, and I shall deceive the Queen once more, by giving her in your stead a young hind.”'),6,0,518,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('31339bf4-23b6-48f6-afbb-aa78d47f2af1','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',11,unistr('For she thought them dead ever since they had been taken away without her knowledge.\u000a\u000a“No, no, madam” (cried the poor clerk of the kitchen, all in tears); “you shall not die, and yet you shall see your children again; but then you must go home with me to my lodgings, where I have concealed them, and I shall deceive the Queen once more, by giving her in your stead a young hind.”\u000a\u000aUpon this he forthwith conducted her to his chamber, where, leaving her to embrace her children, and cry along with them, he went and dressed a young hind, which the Queen had for her supper, and devoured it with the same appetite as if it had been the young Queen. Exceedingly was she delighted with her cruelty, and she had invented a story to tell the King, at his return, how the mad wolves had eaten up the Queen his wife and her two children.\u000a\u000aOne evening, as she was, according to her custom, rambling round about the courts and yards of the palace to see if she could smell any fresh meat, she heard, in a ground room, little Day crying, for his mamma was going to whip him, because he had been naughty; and she heard, at the same time, little Morning begging pardon for her brother.\u000a\u000aThe Ogress presently knew the voice of the Queen and her children, and being quite mad that she had been thus deceived, she commanded next morning, by break of day (with a most horrible voice, which made everybody tremble), that they should bring into the middle'),6,1695,359,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('b86c911b-c787-4adb-a58c-67f31b92438e','ea91a541-7558-4e56-828f-188bdf62df4e',12,unistr('of the great court a large tub, which she caused to be filled with toads, vipers, snakes, and all sorts of serpents, in order to have thrown into it the Queen and her children, the clerk of the kitchen, his wife and maid; all whom she had given orders should be brought thither with their hands tied behind them.\u000a\u000aThey were brought out accordingly, and the executioners were just going to throw them into the tub, when the King (who was not so soon expected) entered the court on horseback (for he came post) and asked, with the utmost astonishment, what was the meaning of that horrible spectacle.\u000a\u000aNo one dared to tell him, when the Ogress, all enraged to see what had happened, threw herself head foremost into the tub, and was instantly devoured by the ugly creatures she had ordered to be thrown into it for others. The King could not but be very sorry, for she was his mother; but he soon comforted himself with his beautiful wife and his pretty children.\u000a\u000aPublic domain. From The Blue Fairy Book (ed. Andrew Lang, 1889); Project Gutenberg ebook #503.'),7,0,264,'','2026-05-28 21:13:57');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('82a38e25-167f-4378-b1c5-3396ca741427','67f13e4c-507e-43fe-91e6-42053bca3ed6',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Sleeping Beauty\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aSleeping Beauty is a classic fairy tale that revolves around a princess who is cursed to fall into a deep sleep until awakened by true love''s kiss. The story explores themes of love, fate, and the passage of time.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- **Cursed Sleep**: The central element of the tale, where the princess is put into a deep slumber by a malevolent force.\u000a- **True Love**: The awakening of the princess is contingent upon the arrival of true love, highlighting the power of love and destiny.\u000a- **Time Passage**: The narrative often includes a significant passage of time, emphasizing the inevitability of fate and the changes it brings.\u000a- **Fairy Tale Elements**: Incorporates magical beings, such as fairies, and fantastical settings that are typical of fairy tales.\u000a- **Moral Lessons**: Often conveys moral lessons about love, patience, and the consequences of actions.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe theme of Sleeping Beauty is prevalent in various cultures and adaptations, illustrating the universal appeal of love conquering adversity. It serves as a metaphor for the transformative power of love and the inevitability of fate, making it a significant narrative in the broader context of fairy tales and folklore.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf'),1,0,340,'Sleeping Beauty > Sources','2026-05-28 21:14:06');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('f464d327-f6b0-4051-8cb6-64edd51b149f','f1f3bead-456d-47a6-a554-c0ef06790fc3',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [Little Red Riding Hood.pdf, The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Fairy Tales\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aFairy tales are a genre of folklore that typically feature magical elements, fantastical creatures, and moral lessons. They often reflect cultural values and societal norms through their narratives.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Inclusion of magical or supernatural elements\u000a- Moral lessons or ethical dilemmas\u000a- Simplistic characters, often archetypal (e.g., heroes, villains)\u000a- A clear distinction between good and evil\u000a- Settings that are often in a mythical or fantastical realm\u000a- Use of repetition and rhyme in storytelling\u000a- Frequently feature fantastical creatures such as fairies, ogres, and enchanted beings\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aFairy tales are part of a broader tradition of storytelling that spans cultures and generations. They serve not only as entertainment but also as a means to convey important life lessons and cultural values. The story of "Little Red Riding Hood" exemplifies these characteristics, illustrating the dangers of naivety and the importance of caution, while also reflecting societal norms regarding trust and the nature of strangers.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Little Red Riding Hood.pdf\u000a- The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf\u000a---'),1,0,316,'Fairy Tales > Sources','2026-05-28 21:14:09');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('9fe361ab-a52c-4f2a-8c78-63a3527d9955','1d4811b2-85cb-4bc0-abee-affc1005242a',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Ogress\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aAn ogress is a character type in folklore that represents a female ogre, often depicted as cruel and monstrous. She embodies themes of greed and the darker aspects of motherhood.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Female counterpart to the ogre.\u000a- Often portrayed as cruel and monstrous.\u000a- Represents themes of greed and negative maternal instincts.\u000a- Commonly found in various folklore and fairy tales.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe concept of the ogress is part of a broader tradition of mythical creatures in folklore that serve to illustrate moral lessons, societal fears, and the complexities of human nature. Ogresses often play antagonistic roles in stories, highlighting the dangers associated with unchecked desires and the darker sides of femininity.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf'),1,0,220,'Ogress > Sources','2026-05-28 21:14:11');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('0b2b45ae-3153-48f6-979b-ffe8823fad04','cb20b925-a06b-4056-8ff5-0bbe5fe2bab5',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [theme]\u000asources: [The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Magic and Enchantment\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aMagic and enchantment are central elements in fairy tales that serve as catalysts for plot progression and character development, often embodying both benevolent and malevolent forces.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- **Supernatural Elements**: Involves spells, curses, and magical beings that influence the narrative.\u000a- **Dual Nature**: Represents both positive (benevolent) and negative (malevolent) forces that affect characters'' fates.\u000a- **Transformation**: Often leads to significant changes in characters or situations, such as physical transformations or altered realities.\u000a- **Conflict and Resolution**: Drives the central conflict and often provides the means for resolution through magical interventions.\u000a- **Symbolism**: Frequently symbolizes deeper themes such as love, fear, and the struggle between good and evil.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aMagic and enchantment are foundational themes in the broader domain of fairy tales and folklore, serving to illustrate moral lessons, cultural values, and the complexities of human nature. These elements not only enhance the fantastical aspects of stories but also reflect societal beliefs and fears, making them integral to the storytelling tradition.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf'),1,0,336,'Magic and Enchantment > Sources','2026-05-28 21:14:15');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('33b04cee-0be9-4e58-b743-9d18df60c92e','67e7e799-b535-47db-8f40-1c92c9a45a98',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# The Blue Fairy Book\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Blue Fairy Book is a collection of fairy tales compiled by Andrew Lang, first published in 1889. It is part of Lang''s series of colored fairy books and is notable for its inclusion of classic tales such as ''The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.''\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Compiled by Andrew Lang, a prominent figure in folklore studies.\u000a- Contains a variety of fairy tales from different cultures.\u000a- Includes well-known stories, contributing to the canon of children''s literature.\u000a- Part of a series of colored fairy books, each with its own thematic focus.\u000a- Aimed at both children and adults, promoting moral lessons and cultural heritage.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe Blue Fairy Book is significant in the study of folklore and children''s literature, as it helped popularize fairy tales and made them accessible to a wider audience. It reflects the Victorian interest in folklore and the importance of storytelling in cultural education. The book has influenced subsequent adaptations and interpretations of fairy tales in various media.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf'),1,0,296,'The Blue Fairy Book > Sources','2026-05-28 21:14:17');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('6562d947-552f-4cda-bd12-0b144975013b','32454a06-9101-46e6-a8c6-0ca97f3198aa',0,unistr('# The Sleeping Beauty In The Wood\u000a\u000a**Source:** The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf | **Type:** pdf | **Pages:** 7 | **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a\u000a## Summary\u000a\u000aThe story of ''The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood'' narrates the tale of a princess cursed by an uninvited fairy to fall into a deep sleep for a hundred years, only to be awakened by a prince. The narrative explores themes of love, fate, and the consequences of envy, culminating in the eventual triumph of love over adversity. The tale also includes elements of magic, such as fairies and enchanted objects, and addresses the darker aspects of human nature through the character of the ogress queen.\u000a\u000a## Related Concepts\u000a\u000a- [Sleeping Beauty](../concepts/sleeping-beauty.md)\u000a- [Fairy Tales](../concepts/fairy-tales.md)\u000a- [Ogress](../concepts/ogress.md)\u000a- [Magic and Enchantment](../concepts/magic-and-enchantment.md)\u000a- [The Blue Fairy Book](../concepts/the-blue-fairy-book.md)\u000a\u000a## Source Information\u000a\u000a- **File:** The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf\u000a- **Type:** pdf\u000a- **Pages:** 7\u000a- **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a- **Parser:** opendataloader\u000a\u000a[^1]: The Sleeping Beauty in the Wood.pdf'),1,0,281,'The Sleeping Beauty In The Wood > Source Information','2026-05-28 21:14:17');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('c62bc67a-f038-4bd8-bd23-b83b505851b8','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',0,unistr('## Part One\u000a\u000aOnce upon a time there was a little princess… She was called Snow White because she was white as snow. Her lips were red as blood. And her hair was black as night. She was a beautiful girl, sweet and gentle. She lived in a palace with her father and her stepmother: the King and the Queen. The Queen was a beautiful woman. But she was wicked and vain.\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile2.png)\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile3.png)\u000a\u000a*Every day, the Queen looked in her magic mirror and asked:*\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000aAnd every day, the mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“You, my Queen! You are! You are the most beautiful of all.”\u000a\u000aTime passed and little Snow White became a beautiful young lady. Everybody loved her. One morning, when the Queen asked her magic mirror:\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000a16 17'),2,0,228,'Part One','2026-05-28 21:14:20');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('2f3448ce-c126-49a3-834b-4191c95beb79','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',1,unistr('![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile4.png)\u000a\u000aThe mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“My Queen, you are beautiful, it’s true! But Snow White is more beautiful than you.”\u000a\u000aThe Queen was furious! She planned a horrible crime. She called a huntsman and commanded him: “Take Snow White deep into the forest… and kill her!” “But… Your Majesty! The beloved Princess!” he said. “Kill Snow White! And take out her heart,” the Queen commanded again.\u000a\u000a## Part Two\u000a\u000aThe huntsman took Snow White deep into the woods. “Forgive me, my Princess… I have orders from the Queen to kill you! But I can’t do that!” he said. “Oh, good man!” she cried, “I’ll run into the forest and I’ll never come back!” Snow White ran and ran through the dark forest. She was so scared! Then, she found a little house. The door was open. She went in.\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile5.png)\u000a\u000a18 19'),3,0,222,'Part Two','2026-05-28 21:14:20');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('bafaf191-2896-4e83-90fb-743f8169d046','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',2,unistr('![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile6.png)\u000a\u000aIn the morning, she woke up and saw the seven masters of the house looking at her. They were seven dwarfs. “Who are you?” they asked. “My name is Snow White,” she answered. And she told them the whole story about the wicked Queen. “Princess Snow White, you can stay,” the Dwarfs said. “Can you help us cook and clean?” “Yes! I can cook and I can wash. And I can make beds,” she said happily. “Please, remember: don’t open the door to strangers!” said the Dwarfs. “The Queen will find you.”\u000a\u000a*A little table was set for dinner: seven little chairs, seven little bowls, seven little spoons, seven little knives, seven little forks, and seven little mugs. Snow White was hungry and thirsty. She thought: “I’ll have a little bread from each bowl. And I’ll drink a little wine from each mug.” Then, feeling so tired, she lay down on one of the seven little beds. And she fell asleep.*\u000a\u000a20 21'),4,0,237,'','2026-05-28 21:14:20');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('c283a44f-0ac5-42a5-83f8-f139ec25f04c','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',3,unistr('## Part Three\u000a\u000aAt the Palace, the Queen asked her magic mirror:\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000aThe mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“My Queen, you are beautiful, it’s true! But Snow White is more beautiful than you. In the mountains, she dwells And she is still alive and well.”\u000a\u000aThe Queen was absolutely furious! “I will kill Snow White myself!” she thought. She made a deadly poison. Then, she put the poison in a beautiful red apple.\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile7.png)\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile8.png)\u000a\u000aThe Queen, dressed in old clothes, went to the little house in the mountains. She knocked on the door, calling: “APPLES! JUICY APPLES! APPLES FOR SALE!” Snow White answered: “I’m sorry, madam. I can’t open the door to strangers.” “Oh, I’m just an old woman! Open the window and take one apple,” said the Queen. Snow White opened the window, took the apple and had one bite. She fell dead to the floor! “Now, I’m the most beautiful of all. Ha, ha, ha!” the Queen said and she ran away.\u000a\u000a23'),5,0,267,'Part Three','2026-05-28 21:14:20');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('faf0d627-e144-4007-b87c-c821fdc42eab','eb0beeee-9e7a-4f2c-b37c-04bfe4fdc347',4,unistr('![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile9.png)\u000a\u000aBack at the Palace, the Queen ran to her magic mirror:\u000a\u000a“Mirror, mirror on the wall, Who’s the fairest of all?”\u000a\u000aThe mirror answered:\u000a\u000a“You, my Queen! You are! You are the most beautiful of all.”\u000a\u000aThe wicked Queen was happy now because the magic mirror never lied.\u000a\u000a## Part Four\u000a\u000aThe Seven Dwarfs found Snow White dead on the floor. In her honor, they made her a glass coffin and placed it on top of a mountain. One day, a young Prince stopped to see her. And he fell in love with Snow White. With a kiss, she came back to a new life with her Prince. Snow White and the Prince had a big wedding. Everyone was invited to the celebration. Even the wicked Queen! And that night, when the Queen saw Snow White, she choked with envy and hate. And she fell dead to the floor. The Prince and the Princess lived happily ever after…\u000a\u000a### THE END\u000a\u000a![image](Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs_images/imageFile10.png)\u000a\u000a24 25'),6,0,243,'Part Four > THE END','2026-05-28 21:14:20');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('7a605760-36bb-4512-a555-1885b08fc4b2','f48ff4bf-1ea5-4876-af44-08f956c8a4ea',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Snow White\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aSnow White is a central character in a classic fairy tale known for her beauty and kindness. Her story has been adapted into various forms of literature and film, making her an iconic figure in popular culture.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Renowned for her exceptional beauty.\u000a- Embodies kindness and innocence.\u000a- Central figure in the tale involving her escape from a wicked queen.\u000a- Associated with seven dwarfs who aid her in her journey.\u000a- Represents themes of jealousy, friendship, and the triumph of good over evil.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aSnow White''s narrative is part of the broader tradition of fairy tales that explore moral lessons and human virtues. Her story has influenced countless adaptations in literature, film, and other media, solidifying her status as a timeless character in storytelling.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),1,0,236,'Snow White > Sources','2026-05-28 21:14:28');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('59afa1bb-edac-4ef7-90ac-c12275ef2ddc','2f85d8b5-9aeb-4316-ac0c-576dfe4934ba',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# The Seven Dwarfs\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Seven Dwarfs are fictional characters from the fairy tale "Snow White," known for their small stature and distinct personalities. They serve as loyal protectors of Snow White, embodying themes of friendship and loyalty.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Each dwarf has a unique name and personality, often reflecting their traits (e.g., Doc, Grumpy, Happy).\u000a- They live together in a cottage in the woods, symbolizing camaraderie and teamwork.\u000a- The dwarfs are characterized by their dedication to Snow White, showcasing their protective nature.\u000a- They play a crucial role in the narrative by defending Snow White from the evil Queen.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe Seven Dwarfs are integral to the story of "Snow White," a classic fairy tale that explores themes of good versus evil, innocence, and the importance of friendship. Their role highlights the significance of loyalty and support in overcoming adversity, making them memorable figures in popular culture and adaptations of the tale.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),1,0,282,'The Seven Dwarfs > Sources','2026-05-28 21:14:33');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('88b215f1-c2be-4f5d-a84d-505168f18387','79c1f417-c3a5-4ee4-a2b7-1b8009f70bbc',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [entity]\u000asources: [Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# The Wicked Queen\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Wicked Queen is the primary antagonist in the fairy tale "Snow White," representing the darker aspects of human nature, particularly vanity and jealousy. Her obsession with being the fairest in the land drives her to commit heinous acts against her stepdaughter, Snow White.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Embodies themes of vanity and jealousy.\u000a- Utilizes dark magic and deception to achieve her goals.\u000a- Portrayed as a beautiful yet malevolent figure.\u000a- Ultimately meets her demise due to her own malicious actions.\u000a- Represents the archetype of the evil stepmother in fairy tales.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe Wicked Queen serves as a cautionary figure within the broader context of fairy tales, illustrating the consequences of unchecked vanity and jealousy. Her character highlights the moral lessons often found in folklore, where good ultimately triumphs over evil, reinforcing the values of kindness and humility.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),1,0,265,'The Wicked Queen > Sources','2026-05-28 21:14:36');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('e23a83d7-db94-444e-94ba-188a6d58fec7','fe2acff7-ce61-4314-ab82-08e81663c4ad',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [instrument]\u000asources: [Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Magic Mirror\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Magic Mirror is a mystical instrument featured in the fairy tale "Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs," serving as a source of truth and revelation for the Queen regarding her beauty and status.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Provides the Queen with information about her beauty relative to others.\u000a- Acts as a catalyst for the Queen''s jealousy and actions against Snow White.\u000a- Functions as a symbol of vanity and the quest for superiority.\u000a- Reflects the inner desires and insecurities of the characters involved.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aIn the broader context of fairy tales, the Magic Mirror represents the theme of self-perception and the dangers of vanity. It highlights the consequences of obsession with beauty and the lengths one may go to maintain a sense of superiority, thus driving the narrative of "Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs."\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),1,0,246,'Magic Mirror > Sources','2026-05-28 21:14:38');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('48cdeb4e-43f3-421f-8f6d-470d121bafa2','2f33fdde-9e54-414d-91cf-eb91dd404580',0,unistr('---\u000atags: [instrument]\u000asources: [Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf]\u000a---\u000a\u000a# Poisoned Apple\u000a\u000a## Definition\u000aThe Poisoned Apple is a pivotal plot device in the fairy tale "Snow White," representing the deceit and treachery of the Evil Queen who uses it in her attempt to eliminate Snow White.\u000a\u000a## Key Characteristics\u000a- Symbolizes deceit and betrayal.\u000a- Serves as a catalyst for the story''s conflict.\u000a- Represents the lengths to which the Queen will go to achieve her goals.\u000a- Acts as a physical manifestation of the Queen''s malice.\u000a\u000a## Context\u000aThe Poisoned Apple is integral to the narrative of "Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs," illustrating themes of jealousy, the struggle between good and evil, and the consequences of vanity. It highlights the dangers of trust and the impact of malicious intentions within fairy tales.\u000a\u000a## Sources\u000a- Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),1,0,218,'Poisoned Apple > Sources','2026-05-28 21:14:40');
INSERT INTO document_chunks VALUES('186ddd7d-748b-43ac-bd54-257053668c49','636ec000-a462-48da-97d2-4441e9e4c468',0,unistr('# Snow White And The Seven Dwarfs\u000a\u000a**Source:** Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf | **Type:** pdf | **Pages:** 6 | **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a\u000a## Summary\u000a\u000aThe story of Snow White follows a beautiful princess who is envied by her wicked stepmother, the Queen. After the Queen orders her death, Snow White escapes into the forest and finds refuge with seven dwarfs. Ultimately, the Queen''s jealousy leads to her downfall, while Snow White is revived by a prince''s kiss, leading to their happy ending.\u000a\u000a## Related Concepts\u000a\u000a- [Snow White](../concepts/snow-white.md)\u000a- [The Seven Dwarfs](../concepts/the-seven-dwarfs.md)\u000a- [The Wicked Queen](../concepts/the-wicked-queen.md)\u000a- [Magic Mirror](../concepts/magic-mirror.md)\u000a- [Poisoned Apple](../concepts/poisoned-apple.md)\u000a\u000a## Source Information\u000a\u000a- **File:** Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf\u000a- **Type:** pdf\u000a- **Pages:** 6\u000a- **Ingested:** 2026-05-28\u000a- **Parser:** opendataloader\u000a\u000a[^1]: Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.pdf'),1,0,241,'Snow White And The Seven Dwarfs > Source Information','2026-05-28 21:14:40');
